#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106651} :activity/id #uuid "eb407cdf-9aea-5548-a574-970269d4ce5b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048184 :topic/id #uuid "86927b03-ba99-5587-9673-31a5bae6ef0b" :topic/math-academy-id 1317} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107178 :tutorial/content "The shape below consists of $6$ line segments defined by the $6$ points $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$, and $F.$ These line segments form a loop. \n\n![](images/25/259bfd166a7870bd1a057b28d4b3324f3b98d05222596f1426afa01d6573e3d8.png)\n\nA chain of straight line segments that form a loop is called a **polygon**.\n\nThe line segments are the **sides** of the polygon, and the endpoints of the sides are the **vertices**.\n\nFor this polygon:\n\n- The $6$ vertices are $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$, and $F.$\n\n- The $6$ sides are $\\overline{AB}$, $\\overline{BC}$, $\\overline{CD}$, $\\overline{DE}$, $\\overline{EF}$, and $\\overline{AF}.$\n\nTo denote a particular polygon, we start at one vertex and loop around the polygon, reading off the vertices in order. So, the polygon above can be denoted $ABCDEF.$\n\nThe singular form of the word *vertices* is **vertex**. So, $A$ is a vertex, $B$ is a vertex, etc." :tutorial/id #uuid "342100ff-c1a7-531c-90a6-e07a80e83fda" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9674 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5fbe01e1-2066-5d27-b53a-a2fa74fc43e6" :step/math-academy-id 188116 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106652}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107179 :tutorial/content "The name of a polygon is determined by the number of sides it has. \n\nThe polygon below has $6$ sides. A six-sided polygon is called a **hexagon**.\n\n![](images/25/259bfd166a7870bd1a057b28d4b3324f3b98d05222596f1426afa01d6573e3d8.png)\n\nThe names of the polygons with up to ten sides are given below:\n\n| Number of sides | Name |\n| --- | --- |\n| $3$ | Triangle |\n| $4$ | Quadrilateral |\n| $5$ | Pentagon |\n| $6$ | Hexagon |\n| $7$ | Heptagon |\n| $8$ | Octagon |\n| $9$ | Nonagon |\n| $10$ | Decagon |" :tutorial/id #uuid "9aaf4a28-03c1-57ea-adcd-968b00ad8a41" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9675 :tutorial/title "Naming Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "d4d97dfe-283b-5019-b2e4-5d6ab6550b1d" :step/math-academy-id 188117 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106653}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105754 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105491 :question/id #uuid "3f0d9dbb-448f-5e1e-b06c-4b6a3a674b09" :question/math-academy-id "e-15616"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9fd1d3c-8479-5b35-abbc-9664cea2e1a8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049350}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442727}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "5e398f9a-e437-5d98-bf9e-cb85ba65689b" :step/math-academy-id 188118 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106654}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107182 :tutorial/content "We can categorize polygons in different ways.\n\nA polygon is **simple** if its boundary does not intersect itself. Otherwise, the polygon is **complex**.\n\n![](images/71/71ef7dbf87cb3a097ec153eb4c4e18920c2567e6a239a788f4340a0a5f33ea6f.png)\n\nA polygon is **convex** if it has no angles pointing inwards. Otherwise, if the polygon has angles pointing inwards, we call it **concave**.\n\n![](images/ef/ef7357c0e21760c3e7f159c253c527e35aac869ce16381132a53356dc53455e2.png)\n\nIt might help to remember that a con**cave** polygon looks a bit like the entrance to a **cave** when viewed from the outside. \n\n![](images/11/11e9844e49a5c586ec573e08865eb7d02dd68459e90df90749116a9eb1575430.png)\n\n**Note**: A more technical way to define a convex polygon is to say that all of its *internal angles* are less than $180^\\circ.$ If you're not sure what an internal angle is yet, don't worry! We'll learn more about the internal angles of a polygon in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "b7c27ce6-fd4a-5560-bc80-0e72700ff320" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9678 :tutorial/title "Types of Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "e39203ba-5fa0-58f6-9ad6-4003cf7c5c77" :step/math-academy-id 188121 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105494 :question/id #uuid "d56604cb-e46a-5d52-94c4-dea5e8c0df3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-15623"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d25a77b-7977-50b2-b375-51b8539bc9b9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442722}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Type of a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "ac8b8e7b-3a84-5a11-ab36-35cd5a021aa5" :step/math-academy-id 188122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107180 :tutorial/content "Consider the pentagon $ABCDE,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b11b509e881cd813ff6879a0a6fa9559d51ffcbc5da4c150a0eaf0a9ee82a159.png)\n\nWe have the following definitions: \n\n- Two *sides* are **adjacent** (or **consecutive**) if they have a common vertex.\n\nFor example, $\\color[rgb]{0.753,0.494,0.643}\\overline{BC}$ is adjacent to $\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}\\overline{AB}$ *and* ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}\\overline{CD}}.$\n\n![](images/76/760a9cbccd117214d4e93392cdae17bf117497501b05f7b2e25c6c8cb5cd43e1.png)\n\n- Two *vertices* are **adjacent** if there is a side joining them. \n\nFor example, the vertex $E$ is adjacent to the vertices $D$ and $A$.\n\n![](images/6d/6d857920609da14fb0b31f762400c5f54e9e428717c2b55fcb17215280bbbfed.png)\n\n- A **diagonal** is a line segment that joins two non-adjacent vertices. \n\nFor example, ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{AC}}}, {\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{BD}}},$ and ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{BE}}}$ are diagonals of our pentagon.\n\n![](images/ad/adf7afaa66144a33dba7ff3732b01619bc13cac0fae59c466393d4084c748a59.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "b82f1900-5686-5fcc-8354-6c00b102f576" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9676 :tutorial/title "The Parts of a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "0d4447dd-331d-5e2c-9e2b-470035c668cb" :step/math-academy-id 188125 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105756 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105492 :question/id #uuid "e72f5a3b-8085-54dc-a09a-121925ef509c" :question/math-academy-id "e-15617"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61c58110-9813-5588-81d2-5735718589aa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527176}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Adjacent Vertices and Sides"} :step/id #uuid "a35aa384-e796-5e75-adfa-23d8efbf0c06" :step/math-academy-id 188126 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105757 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105493 :question/id #uuid "33bf6630-1558-50b9-8823-0bd58d0787af" :question/math-academy-id "e-15618"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed0dc2a-7c76-5fee-9aa8-aad529cf1952" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442729}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "94356407-759e-59ef-b5e4-6f11d01f5cf9" :step/math-academy-id 188129}] :activity/title "Polygons" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105971}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199284} :activity/id #uuid "ea08cd43-7cea-59a6-a6eb-b25618d785bd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048429 :topic/id #uuid "9eaae820-5193-5702-9ef4-efac567af4b5" :topic/math-academy-id 4136} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199284 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199466 :tutorial/content "Every division problem can be written as a fraction and vice-versa. In this lesson, we'll extend this idea to division problems involving negative numbers.\n\nTo begin, let's find a fraction that's equivalent to the following division problem:\n\n$$\n(-5) \\div 9\n$$\n\nNotice that we are dividing a negative number ${(\\color{red}{-5}})$ by a positive one $({\\color{blue}{9}}).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n![](images/68/6816c161288078cb818415f78c12133bae8aa85933eae82f74f6601b83142c35.svg)\n\nTo write down the fraction that goes in the box, recall that \n\n$$\n5 \\div 9 = \\dfrac{5}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-5)} \\div {\\color{blue}9} = {\\color{red}-\\dfrac{5}{9}}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "331c6a05-6da8-51c8-84ad-b69ff3dd9f56" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7635 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "77d5039f-8317-56ed-9c58-9503a2ff03cc" :step/math-academy-id 44285 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199285}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199285 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198381 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198023 :question/id #uuid "41696b59-d81e-5c49-a967-141f67934aa1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12359"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65a25c72-9fa7-58fc-bf45-9cc2761696ea" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050719}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447644}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Division Problems as Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "04ca83ac-7a80-5eb8-8fb9-98dc863ddddb" :step/math-academy-id 44286 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199286}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199286 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198382 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198024 :question/id #uuid "e40e5f9e-aab7-53ed-8850-bd855f2b97a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12360"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fe851f3-482c-5426-87b2-32ec3c1b40f6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048768}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531656}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Division Problems as Mixed Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "68dba314-3daa-559b-9888-94f883e881ef" :step/math-academy-id 44287 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199288}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199287 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198383 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198025 :question/id #uuid "a650b9e6-c97c-5471-af2d-76a5cfb0caff" :question/math-academy-id "e-12361"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d17ce34f-7d65-5f44-a1c0-dfd3c2ea55f6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447643}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalent Fractions With Negative Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "af22cc6e-47b4-5a18-a2b1-50d51596813e" :step/math-academy-id 44288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199467 :tutorial/content "What is the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{41}{8} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[1pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{-8}\n$$\n\nA negative number can be obtained if we divide two numbers, one of which is positive and the other negative.\n\nSo, given a negative fraction, we have the following equivalent notations:\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{41}{8} = \\dfrac{-41}{8} = \\dfrac{41}{-8}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we see that the denominator on the right-hand side is negative $({{\\color{red}-8}}).$ Therefore, the missing numerator must be $({{\\color{blue}41}}){:}$\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{41}{8} = \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\color{blue}41}}{-8}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "652d8025-fc3e-56b0-ac6a-10bc44ecaf15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7636 :tutorial/title "Equivalent Fractions With Negative Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "c1d49b55-ca98-5e16-a9de-14e2aaf4fe80" :step/math-academy-id 44289 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199287}}] :activity/title "Equivalent Fractions With Negative Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198482}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074634} :activity/id #uuid "4417fe29-8f51-5120-ac66-9aaeadadb6f3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508 :topic/id #uuid "a587db09-9578-5d61-b811-99a95c85061d" :topic/math-academy-id 553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075034 :tutorial/content "We can find **equivalent ratios** by multiplying or dividing all numbers in a ratio by the same value. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following ratio:\n\n$$\n2:6\n$$\n\nIf we multiply *both* numbers by $5,$ for example, we get the ratio $10:30.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,6 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓\\times \\,5 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓\\times \\,5 &  \\\\ \\,10 &  & : &  & \\,30 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratios $2:6$ and $10:30$ are equivalent. \n\nSimilarly, if we take the ratio $2:6$ and divide *both* numbers by $2,$ we get $1:3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,6 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓÷\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratios  $2:6$ and  $1:3$ are also equivalent." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc2c9ce9-c2b0-5d68-81db-b8b0273407f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8203 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6aa20fa4-3163-5025-84f7-a1ddd4324cd0" :step/math-academy-id 186681 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074635}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075035 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the following two ratios are equivalent:\n\n![](images/c6/c673bfcd099ef612f48bd0da7f8535cb9406d23088392e056884fb9415bb5100.svg)\n\nWhat is the missing number?\n\nTo find a ratio equivalent to $3 : 5,$ we multiply or divide both numbers by the same value. \n\nIn this case, we need to get $12$ from ${\\color{blue}{3}},$ so we multiply ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ by $4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,5 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓\\times \\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓\\times \\,4 &  \\\\ 12 &  & : &  & \\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{0}[/math]}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to get ![](images/50/50f3c1b8dc3b1628130d1de11abcc455fc22006f32b7486f658c8134fcc3221c.svg) from ${\\color{red}{5}},$ we multiply ${\\color{red}{5}}$ by $4$ as well: \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\times 4 = \\bbox[3px,lightgray]{\\color{red}20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $20.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3539d419-9d5a-5467-8a88-7ef318329859" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8204 :tutorial/title "Finding Missing Values in Equivalent Ratios"} :step/id #uuid "4854b84c-59a0-5a98-a9c6-9b8427de8ab8" :step/math-academy-id 186682 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073738 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073447 :question/id #uuid "a453e80d-016f-5a6c-9890-705463eb1fb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13120"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ac1e54-c949-5ffe-b377-b0e35240a13c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050924}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518125}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalent Ratios"} :step/id #uuid "55be2711-44d0-588a-899f-562ac0853236" :step/math-academy-id 186683 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075036 :tutorial/content "We can simplify ratios by dividing both numbers in the ratio by a common factor.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the following ratio: \n\n$$\n18:9\n$$\n\nThe numbers $18$ and $9$ share a common factor of $9,$ so we can divide both numbers by $9$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,18 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,9 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,2 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18:9$ is equivalent to $2:1.$ \n\nSince $2$ and $1$ have no common factors, $2:1$ is the simplest form of this ratio. We often prefer to work with ratios in their simplest form, where possible." :tutorial/id #uuid "c7ece136-e063-594d-b25d-4cf19bca7890" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8205 :tutorial/title "Expressing Ratios in Their Simplest Form"} :step/id #uuid "23872027-241e-5f61-8492-76236e405360" :step/math-academy-id 186686 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073739 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073448 :question/id #uuid "0a57949c-efc9-5403-9e7b-08346153f184" :question/math-academy-id "e-13121"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae17eb3f-f444-5ab3-82fd-6c92b97b0e41" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074371} {:d