89626} {:answer/id #uuid "640e11a1-45f1-5bd9-ba70-d2bd9afff6a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489750} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8d1a24-f0b3-5957-bac1-4b551d6b4aff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489770} {:answer/id #uuid "736365bf-c5ec-516a-96d3-5dcf4aad4bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489785} {:answer/id #uuid "8790ecbf-d319-56cd-88f6-19ef7c84fee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489626} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7379773-3775-53b0-a3c8-4af0a853a08a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ddf5db22-7c12-5596-b4f6-5a61a8187c11" :question/math-academy-id "q-59828" :question/problem "If $10$ children ate $15$ cakes, what number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of eaten cakes to children is $3:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $3$ eaten cakes for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ children." :question/worked-solution "Since $10$ children can eat $15$ cakes, the ratio of cakes to children is $15:10.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,15 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,10 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,5 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,5 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,3 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $3:2$ is equivalent to $15:10.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of eaten cakes to children is $3:2\\,$ because there are $3$ cakes for every $2\\,$ children.\"\n\nThe missing number is $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "2b306b5b-d4d0-5bd9-ad85-47853cadb9b4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490388}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490347 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab0f3fb-4e36-5cb7-9b92-bf6665005eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2:1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489538} {:answer/id #uuid "4b95827d-b1f3-513f-a04b-4e3930a7a8ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2:2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489690} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2c18ff-74f1-5d6b-965e-9c4142e35106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1:4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489857} {:answer/id #uuid "a3480f70-f3e4-5b52-9b49-7446ba7d2f81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1:2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489878} {:answer/id #uuid "c8575184-e89c-5e39-8b80-accc5525b223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2:6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489949}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489878} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2e569b9-68b5-54d0-bd0f-496bb3345979" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ec0c1b9c-5490-5482-a264-c0fe31855e42" :question/math-academy-id "q-101444" :question/problem "![](images/ed/eda2132e8e050399298b97dc9d36cf27862d6aa4625e7aa870bb4c8fd246e781.png)\n\nWhat ratio describes the number of triangles to the number of pentagons?" :question/worked-solution "We count the number of triangles. We see that there are $2$ triangles.\n\nWe count the number of pentagons. We see that there are $4$ pentagons.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to pentagons is $2:4.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers in a ratio by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of triangles to pentagons is $1:2.$"} :step/id #uuid "338c847b-f4c7-5b56-ae4c-a5bc33c176b9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490388 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b8636ec-a6ca-5436-8dd7-6da7ca001b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489611} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f1e389-8ab5-503e-b205-cc5f0bcc7461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489916} {:answer/id #uuid "c459ed53-041f-5cf1-a8ab-49b000eb425c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489940} {:answer/id #uuid "cd10f7eb-7f59-5705-9648-668ef168f8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489970} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ee81e2-8914-5cd6-8aba-c7a5b8a811e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489984}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489970} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa08328e-cc55-5205-a1b2-f02f8b69f734" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1816745e-33d2-5605-bad0-d9ea4d6d5feb" :question/math-academy-id "q-59896" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6c5263a440942da90d5ed6dc6a87e9e6caa0848dfb23c1a904eb828dfcaaeb1.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of rectangles to pentagons is $1:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there is $1$ rectangle for every $\\boxed{}$ pentagons." :question/worked-solution "First, we count the number of rectangles. We see that there are $2$ rectangles.\n\nThen, we count the number of pentagons. We see that there are $2$ pentagons.\n\nSo the ratio of rectangles to pentagons is $2:2.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $2:2.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of rectangles to pentagons is $1:1$ because there is $1$ rectangle for every $1$ pentagon.\""} :step/id #uuid "f85a5132-bc8d-5028-bf7c-c2b4e0758ecd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490347}}] :activity/title "Equivalent Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489503}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515630} :activity/id #uuid "fbfe7b63-dd84-5bb9-ae0b-151a90820b22" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508 :topic/id #uuid "a587db09-9578-5d61-b811-99a95c85061d" :topic/math-academy-id 553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cf06951-cbcc-525c-9842-0890581834c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072477} {:answer/id #uuid "b04ecb02-3d9b-5237-9855-274124892856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072478} {:answer/id #uuid "d7352e89-2b19-5a8c-a0e2-f926cfe67ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072479} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf88531-0b03-5971-a795-d3362dd0c20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072480} {:answer/id #uuid "41370d63-4b05-5808-abe3-4ce7f528b248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186072479} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad60804-e649-58c3-9918-6d89f3cb7178" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cd08e83a-7cd3-5ff7-9cbf-8fd4782df22a" :question/math-academy-id "q-59740" :question/problem "![](images/38/3865ec425991f0d8ceae82f002d0096f2fd2ea56743173e754b15472b7c85031.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of triangles to hexagons is $1:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there is $1$ triangle for every $\\boxed{}$ hexagon." :question/worked-solution "First, we count the number of triangles. We see that there are $3$ triangles.\n\nThen, we count the number of hexagons. We see that there are $3$ hexagons.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to hexagons is $3:3.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $3:3.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of triangles to hexagons is $1:1$ because there is $1$ triangle for every $1$ hexagon.\""} :step/id #uuid "11c74a03-1f27-5d00-94fb-dfb867dd0243" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515703}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e450b03-7981-5093-828a-cb8e22ab078c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514940} {:answer/id #uuid "1eea5d3b-05dc-5f21-af4a-69cd688d94b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514942} {:answer/id #uuid "39f0e787-bfeb-525d-962e-fbc0be1dea03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514999} {:answer/id #uuid "a036cbb4-0c7b-5101-b61a-3587c08488d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515214} {:answer/id #uuid "f2cded69-fda8-50a1-9450-6993ad54936e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515382}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514999} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4ada293-0707-5fcb-af41-5176ed9ea4fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2295df98-ce45-53e5-ab4e-ebfbd4881c7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-131918" :question/problem "If there are $21$ pears and $15$ apples, then what number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of pears to apples is $7:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $7$ pears for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ apples." :question/worked-solution "Since there are $21$ pears and $15$ apples, the ratio of pears to apples is $21:15.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,21 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,15 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,7 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $21:15$ is equivalent to $7:5.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is \"The ratio of pears to apples is $7:5\\,$ because there are $7$ pears for every $5\\,$ apples.\"\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $5.$"} :step/id #uuid "9791145a-39f3-503d-85e5-4249e43b05a5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515703 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1034259d-6a50-53d2-8b2a-548dd4a01b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514905} {:answer/id #uuid "58839e93-46a7-5cba-a7a8-b5b186148928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515069} {:answer/id #uuid "9818025a-7109-5370-82e1-53582885ae81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515193} {:answer/id #uuid "a24fea6b-a341-5cf3-808c-27c825cb1528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515218} {:answer/id #uuid "f8464049-28bd-5b10-888e-adf0b228fb7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515394}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515218} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ccbefd-df3d-54b1-82ef-3bcd139c5460" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e345f1f6-4e4b-57f5-a961-226088f10a38" :question/math-academy-id "q-92799" :question/problem "Dominick has $8$ steel marbles and $14$ glass marbles. What number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of steel marbles to glass marbles is $4:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $4$ steel marbles for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ glass marbles." :question/worked-solution "Since there are $8$ steel marbles and $14$ glass marbles, the ratio of steel marbles to glass marbles is $8:14.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,8 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,14 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,4 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $4:7$ is equivalent to $8:14.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of steel marbles to glass marbles is $4:7$ because there are $4$ steel marbles for every $7$ glass marbles.\"\n\nHence, the missing number is $7.$"} :step/id #uuid "e2d088e6-4253-54d2-b40f-9e61dc190ae1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515670}}] :activity/title "Equivalent Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514870}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518283} :activity/id #uuid "24f1cf90-b2d9-5450-9055-e10683bb7bac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508 :topic/id #uuid "a587db09-9578-5d61-b811-99a95c85061d" :topic/math-academy-id 553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518232 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3329434f-8065-5d0b-a666-f563fb8143ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517584} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa0127b-9b67-546b-b18b-95b43306edd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517729} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d821f5-db5e-51a5-910e-5dfafa63518c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517857} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf427e4-c3bd-59be-8e07-eb80d2971207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517872} {:answer/id #uuid "d52ce07d-1be8-57ce-8afa-0dbaac7ec0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517857} :answer-field/id #uuid "2089b399-51c9-5b3c-8aca-f12d0c324c24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "210d98b1-d520-5974-b07e-4d5e5c06b223" :question/math-academy-id "q-59905" :question/problem "There are $8$ students with blonde hair and $12$ students with black hair in a ballet class. What number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $2:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $2$ students with blonde hair for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ students with black hair." :question/worked-solution "Since there are $8$ students with blonde hair and $12$ students with black hair, the ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $8:12.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,8 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,12 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,2 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $2:3$ is equivalent to $8:12.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $2:3\\,$ because there are $2$ students with blonde hair for every $3\\,$ students with black hair.\"\n\nHence, the missing number is $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "27569124-56eb-5998-a405-7cf8748dd2b9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518283 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45301129-1430-561c-975d-43df3220cccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517623} {:answer/id #uuid "6c2a4ee8-1023-57d3-b19d-01bf628ad784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517691} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e6ce96-e241-5d81-839a-77f0c738feb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517798} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f46437-76c7-59da-bffa-d2b285df56bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517805} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c4007a-d8a2-5dec-b9fd-1ec8181c9d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517888}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517623} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0e97fe2-4795-50cd-a7b5-3d08d498d02e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c42ab941-f231-5a97-a430-88c5273648a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-59898" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec8c3585db1981d001864b3c135f84e46643051d22f28e77f82e8b5a92d0f09d.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of squares to circles is $\\boxed{}:1$ because there are $\\boxed{}$ squares for every $1$ circle." :question/worked-solution "First, we count the number of squares. We see that there are $3$ squares.\n\nThen, we count the number of circles. We see that there are $3$ circles.\n\nSo the ratio of squares to circles is $3:3.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $3:3.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of squares to circles is $1:1$ because there is $1$ square for every $1$ circle.\""} :step/id #uuid "b1a71eab-7b52-5146-8e98-812bf70a532c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4100d4ff-ed37-52a8-9450-a287631a77bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517616} {:answer/id #uuid "a6de607f-d322-5729-a80a-a71d1e3f5ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517799} {:answer/id #uuid "be92aa79-3698-5510-9935-1b8212508906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517850} {:answer/id #uuid "d32b53af-b5dd-51b9-bb5d-778d1da7078c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517884} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ab97c6-7fa2-5c7d-b127-0ede64e61ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517897}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517799} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f42b0d-1f1e-5535-971e-bc06dfe092ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f0e3ac9f-3533-517a-898b-d1cc891d8b27" :question/math-academy-id "q-59903" :question/problem "There are $15$ girls and $9$ boys in a geometry class. What number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of girls to boys is $5:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $5$ girls for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ boys." :question/worked-solution "Since there are $15$ girls and $9$ boys, the ratio of girls to boys is $15:9.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,15 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,5 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $5:3$ is equivalent to $15:9.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of girls to boys is $5:3\\,$ because there are $5$ girls for every $3\\,$ boys.\"\n\nHence, the missing number is $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "e6ad2332-1f0b-5002-894a-06b6c20121c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518232}}] :activity/title "Equivalent Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517478}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065135} :activity/id #uuid "457bb695-c5a7-5c4b-bc54-87bb84576ab7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048957 :topic/id #uuid "cf262221-31bc-5e58-a6f2-8acf1c2b4906" :topic/math-academy-id 355} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065367 :tutorial/content "Fractions that contain multiple terms in the numerator and a single term in the denominator can be split into separate terms.\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {x + 2} {3} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac x 3 + \\dfrac 2 3\n\t}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {3a - 4b + 1} {5} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac {3a} 5 - \\dfrac {4b} 5 + \\dfrac 1 5.\n\t}\n$$\n\nOften, we're able to simplify the fractions in the individual terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {10x + 9} {2} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac {10x} {2} + \\dfrac 9 2 &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac {10} {2} \\cdot x + \\dfrac 9 2 &= \\\t\n\t\t5 \\cdot x + \\dfrac 9 2 &= \\\n\t\t5x + \\dfrac 9 2 \n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0a37b6ec-6750-5aea-ae26-ad82d059a0ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3375 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "871a576b-9882-5cab-a267-5bfaae09bb5c" :step/math-academy-id 18870 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065136}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065136 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064257 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064102 :question/id #uuid "6e148f32-5352-5368-a08a-6da278a6068d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5124"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "724bdf2f-c9f0-564e-ae33-c64d44c582ef" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050998}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514455}] :knowledge-point/title "Splitting a Rational Expression With Two Terms in the Numerator"} :step/id #uuid "213c99f3-2e96-5282-8c9f-7798f6d583ee" :step/math-academy-id 18871 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065137}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065137 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064258 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064103 :question/id #uuid "41294f0b-dc6a-5a84-a7f8-7c2370b5733c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5125"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0649943a-643f-592f-8815-45c871f7e7dd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531561}] :knowledge-point/title "Splitting a Rational Expression With Three Terms in the Numerator"} :step/id #uuid "2ca88a28-b372-506f-bc28-472bf491340a" :step/math-academy-id 18872}] :activity/title "Splitting Rational Expressions Into Separate Terms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064444}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514536} :activity/id #uuid "b23c2052-349f-52a5-ba1e-34bdd73733c2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048957 :topic/id #uuid "cf262221-31bc-5e58-a6f2-8acf1c2b4906" :topic/math-academy-id 355} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514499 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03200003-7c03-5877-9799-fe42b38367d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5d}{3}-3e+2f" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513535} {:answer/id #uuid "875b0e1a-c6ee-5156-9b67-b9182be36dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}-3e+2f" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513884} {:answer/id #uuid "a837bab8-9905-5840-bdf4-12b924029d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5d}{3}-3e-2f" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513968} {:answer/id #uuid "b7dfca26-24d1-5b2e-89b8-03b7057f94a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "d-9e-2f" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514016} {:answer/id #uuid "e8af9eea-0d25-50f2-b747-1968474f3c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2d-9e-2f" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513968} :answer-field/id #uuid "5be07d56-1b09-5907-9a4a-651321f7cdec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cedc5783-2073-5a5a-9238-c637979688f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-2595" :question/problem "$\\frac{5d-9e-6f}{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5d-9e-6f}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{5d}{3}-\\frac{9e}{3}-\\frac{6f}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{5d}{3}-\\frac{9}{3}\\cdot e-\\frac{6}{3}\\cdot f & = \\\\ \\frac{5d}{3}-3e-2f & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "500be06c-ef79-5741-b61d-1e169ee21469" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514518}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75c969ca-367d-5068-acbe-f0467f813227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=2$ and $b=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513843} {:answer/id #uuid "c1682820-b145-57af-bb60-67f57aa12777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=2$ and $b=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514043} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8541b1-df31-5bf9-9595-f8be69351a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=8$ and $b=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514063} {:answer/id #uuid "e31a9be0-36c9-524a-9bf7-ae092f66b308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=3$ and $b=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514127} {:answer/id #uuid "f8331f74-2394-5f6e-af1d-324225a8b66a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$a=2$ and $b=-12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514180}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513843} :answer-field/id #uuid "23253a0c-91f5-5d73-8605-1ae876bdd4b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "69e15305-fd39-5e12-9afd-c74d993158ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-2593" :question/problem "If $\\frac{8x-12y}{4}$ is written in the form $ax+by,$ what are the values of $a$ and $b?$" :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8x-12y}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{8x}{4}-\\frac{12y}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{4}\\cdot x-\\frac{12}{4}\\cdot y & = \\\\ 2x-3y & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $ax+by,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{a}x+{{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{b}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$ and $b=-3.$"} :step/id #uuid "7d64cf93-ea34-55d5-ba8a-91d5a0d045c0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514499}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "233176ce-70f9-5e6d-87d1-a9ee42aee061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a-6b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513629} {:answer/id #uuid "2b6d12dd-a924-575c-bf78-e849044273ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513650} {:answer/id #uuid "6a973a8f-68d4-5f16-b9b5-43302aeb1566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4a-\\frac{3b}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513818} {:answer/id #uuid "98adbab9-1df8-5350-9e28-7f78e7ab5f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a-\\frac{3b}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513929} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b993a8-041c-5f04-b5e8-b517b901f0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3b}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514002}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513929} :answer-field/id #uuid "a56dfd75-69de-5099-a23d-5bcbe223963a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b8beba6a-7b91-5602-93f9-62eba4e754b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-2596" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{4a-6b}{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4a-6b}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{4a}{4}-\\frac{6b}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{4}\\cdot a-\\frac{6}{4}\\cdot b & = \\\\ a-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot b & = \\\\ a-\\frac{3b}{2} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "98967ed7-c764-5a5b-b909-edddb93ab4db" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514530}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19980522-9bdd-55a7-8d9d-8828e76405ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6-\\frac{y}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513600} {:answer/id #uuid "320dbf36-2cfb-59da-86bb-c1a4774c9420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4-\\frac{y}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513668} {:answer/id #uuid "c52cd410-949a-5ecc-9db0-e370a7d7839c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514049} {:answer/id #uuid "c6076542-ac8d-521b-91c3-c0f173f61489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\frac{y}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514051} {:answer/id #uuid "db0641d9-8d65-5bc9-86fa-3e03187c823e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514105}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514051} :answer-field/id #uuid "abc45ee2-9c31-50c1-96af-d62cdd130b8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "18e51362-5c55-557f-b76e-006c8ec5e5f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-103607" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{12-3y}{6}?$" :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12-3y}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{12}{6}-\\frac{3y}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{12}{6}-\\frac{3}{6}\\cdot y & = \\\\ 2-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot y & = \\\\ 2-\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c51eee0e-bd4d-55fb-bf8e-c27eef65b158"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514536 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514368 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "000d1ee2-eb0e-516d-880b-59e76d658afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4a}{3}-2b+3c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513525} {:answer/id #uuid "17d560ac-9cc4-58ec-bd8a-4cc87ded49ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{3}-2b+3c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513592} {:answer/id #uuid "3def3569-bf02-567b-a7ce-c0676ebb8e33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4a}{3}-2b-3c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513694} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c1327e-3c63-5535-94ec-15c702bd94af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7abc}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513950} {:answer/id #uuid "d2165713-727d-5608-af21-2766b713333b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4a}{3}+2b-3c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514078}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513525} :answer-field/id #uuid "33f1ff95-b84b-5146-8e81-b9dec5eae7f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1169d5be-77b9-5c01-b29e-d873231e9f86" :question/math-academy-id "q-2591" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{4a-6b+9c}{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4a-6b+9c}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{4a}{3}-\\frac{6b}{3}+\\frac{9c}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{4a}{3}-\\frac{6}{3}\\cdot b+\\frac{9}{3}\\cdot c & = \\\\ \\frac{4a}{3}-2b+3c & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d09296b7-b1c5-5fd5-a919-8913f8b1040a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514516}}] :activity/title "Splitting Rational Expressions Into Separate Terms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513521}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139522} :activity/id #uuid "4e2e5333-ed8e-576e-abed-94cc93efbdb5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049051 :topic/id #uuid "d5e55293-c14f-55db-a945-4aa2b42df552" :topic/math-academy-id 2310} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139662 :tutorial/content "A **compound inequality** is an inequality that consists of multiple different inequalities put together. For example, the compound inequality \n\n$$\nx \\leq 0 \\hspace{.25cm} \\text{ or } \\hspace{.25cm} x \\gt  3\n$$\n\n states that $x$ is less than or equal to $0$, or greater than $3$.\n\nFor the compound \"or\" inequality to be satisfied, at least one of the separate inequalities must be satisfied. To illustrate:\n\n- $x=-1$ satisfies the inequality because it satisfies $x \\leq 0.$\n\n- $x=5$ satisfies the inequality because it satisfies $x > 3.$\n\n- $x=1$ does not satisfy the inequality because it does not satisfy $x \\leq 0$ and it also does not satisfy $x > 3.$\n\nTo graph a compound inequality involving the word \"or,\" we graph the two separate inequalities on the same number line. In this particular case,\n\n- the inequality $x \\leq 0$ consists of a closed circle at $0$ and an arrow to the left, while\n\n- the inequality $x >3$ consists of an open circle at $3$ and an arrow to the right.\n\nThe resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/96/96f1b1ee25ebc210a83ba12d90396683b7e2660050f820c4057ab85de419db91.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "770f70a7-6710-533c-bd9a-e37123e913bb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8132 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "73ffc612-4a54-5b2a-9949-3131ab4b5010" :step/math-academy-id 79780 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139523}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138536 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138073 :question/id #uuid "014723fc-0b7d-5212-a41e-73847f5adb00" :question/math-academy-id "e-13011"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6090342d-3d4e-519f-ab7d-3fb6df6e92a8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050519}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481665}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing \"Or\" Inequalities on Number Lines"} :step/id #uuid "1d905dee-c60b-587c-8b1e-96a234f4883a" :step/math-academy-id 79781 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139524}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139524 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138537 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138075 :question/id #uuid "e5b6a9dc-3a19-5496-9e22-d953ff4eb647" :question/math-academy-id "e-13018"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32daa2fb-e5fa-5e44-b6f2-5d815d9145af" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491986}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying \"Or\" Inequalities Given on Number Lines"} :step/id #uuid "bcf79e23-3398-56f2-9f7c-6dddc11d8b32" :step/math-academy-id 79784 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139525}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139663 :tutorial/content "Compound inequalities can also be written with the word \"and.\" However, such inequalities are often written together as a single expression with two inequality signs. For example, the compound inequality \n\n$$\n-4 \\leq x < -1\n$$\n\n means that $-4 \\leq x$ and $x < -1.$\n\nFor the compound \"and\" inequality to be satisfied, both of the separate inequalities must be satisfied. To illustrate:\n\n- $x=-3$ satisfies the inequality because it satisfies both $-4 \\leq x$ and $x < -1.$\n\n- $x=-5$ does not satisfy the inequality because it does not satisfy $-4 \\leq x.$\n\n- $x=0$ does not satisfy the inequality because it does not satisfy $x < -1.$\n\nTo graph this compound inequality, we first graph the two inequalities separately.\n\n![](images/42/42edf8ee61badbbd1e5525a3843cfbe189e6432b2ad06455c1bdca6a7087ac56.png)\n\nThen, we keep only where they overlap.\n\n![](images/fa/fa07233f96bed30ddfcc5905a55a835763359cd34188e4d1c249c13b1d27d25c.png)\n\n**Note:** Any compound \"and\" inequality will always represent a segment between two points. So, it is not always necessary to draw both inequalities and find the overlap. To speed up the process, we can just draw the endpoints with open or closed circles as appropriate, and highlight the area between the endpoints." :tutorial/id #uuid "9859e4f2-2267-558c-923f-35c348957ad9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8133 :tutorial/title "Compound \"And\" Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "59dbf0dd-b3a9-5867-8cbf-3b28624f9ad9" :step/math-academy-id 79788 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139526}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138538 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138074 :question/id #uuid "21eace75-0b5a-5b37-903c-c238e3821bee" :question/math-academy-id "e-13012"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9106723c-b8b0-586e-8c2b-9bb4f50bc945" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492061}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing \"And\" Inequalities on Number Lines"} :step/id #uuid "7e38370d-1c18-5d27-bb22-cf9444dd1b90" :step/math-academy-id 79789 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139527}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139527 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138539 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138076 :question/id #uuid "347520ff-e759-5d99-acf7-0bc8a8f26689" :question/math-academy-id "e-13019"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a707a741-0208-5a74-8478-ac05ff2673ab" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492032}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying \"And\" Inequalities Given on Number Lines"} :step/id #uuid "40b2748c-2bc7-5064-b788-ba5145c312f9" :step/math-academy-id 79793}] :activity/title "Compound Inequalities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138614}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492094} :activity/id #uuid "9814bdeb-c5c1-549b-8942-0dcd8592f0fd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049051 :topic/id #uuid "d5e55293-c14f-55db-a945-4aa2b42df552" :topic/math-academy-id 2310} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03910583-d566-5a8b-bfc2-8972fe3ecdd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f6/f6139cea308db19681b8e2239e01f77d4af406a728041aa738b29ee3ce6a603e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491454} {:answer/id #uuid "7b650c21-47ed-5214-a59e-48d2075d1b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/90/901ffc463b1516e30690f41af6225c62a1a3cc3950bc34e28bc5b13ae7314b28.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491672} {:answer/id #uuid "8698ddac-ea80-50d9-9ad8-a8742ba97aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/24/2415d02b1c88fbeb254f41d3540b81e20be8fb94da400b77fabca65e40da3b8c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491685} {:answer/id #uuid "97ae2684-9fda-5d9f-bda1-046e9cd84f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6f/6fa6ef603a454d9f219ca5967a87a99cc8afabc30105d36879c172b8385fd36b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491718} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0ec700-72a5-535a-a4a1-e48679183f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/49/496986cb7dca8bffb2d2856f3867e48d794f106139d4703e1b2166d9a223781d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491756}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491454} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9c1885b-25c3-55b9-8caf-90ff3bd587c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d2934ac4-af1a-5b63-bae8-4d35ad544ba0" :question/math-academy-id "q-54810" :question/problem "Which of the following number lines represents the inequality $-3<x<1?$" :question/worked-solution "The inequality $-3<x<1$ means that $x$ can take any value greater than $-3,$ and less than $1.$\n\nTo represent this on a number line, we draw an open circle at $-3$ and another open circle at $1.$ Then, we highlight the region between the points.\n\n![](images/f6/f6139cea308db19681b8e2239e01f77d4af406a728041aa738b29ee3ce6a603e.png)"} :step/id #uuid "1a122adf-7490-5a96-86fc-64fdc36ac5fa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492092}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0158d2c2-46e2-511e-9829-663aca526316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x\\le -1$ or $x\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491450} {:answer/id #uuid "2fd0b12d-c6d3-5074-bd89-cbaa31e2e2ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x<-1$ or $x>1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491548} {:answer/id #uuid "3f38c2dd-7e7e-5407-9e8b-c247c54b9afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x>-1$ or $x>1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491579} {:answer/id #uuid "7853872b-6305-5952-a1e3-1e4da4e83d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x<-1$ or $x<1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491663} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ce2a8a-1854-5692-8ab6-ce6a4ef7ea6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x\\ge -1$ or $x\\le 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491747}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491548} :answer-field/id #uuid "81599ce3-b5fd-5790-a91f-aa92a6b6fa9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "087d9e13-8fc3-51e2-a62c-c006b17d535f" :question/math-academy-id "q-54820" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f2fc2248ce56cd8209d9f6438d24c7f9e9f805f320ef5549be25282cfbaa6e61.png)\n\nWhich inequality corresponds to the highlighted region shown on the number line above?" :question/worked-solution "The red rays contain all values less than  $-1$ or greater than $1.$ In addition,\n\n- the open circle at $-1$ implies that $x$ cannot be equal to $-1,$ and\n\n- the open circle at $1$ implies that $x$ cannot be equal to $1.$\n\nTherefore, the highlighted region represents $x<-1$ or $x>1.$"} :step/id #uuid "471f4a51-0ae6-5b3b-b0f1-91ebb382a932"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492032 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05fd7dc9-4b77-586d-9ed5-f96227aed75b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2<x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491463} {:answer/id #uuid "1b458bce-037d-59e4-a297-afaa3a6e5449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2<x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491508} {:answer/id #uuid "5101b5ff-6f7e-5771-aeaf-1f3ea7c2baa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491607} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb5ca05-d4de-59e6-8172-825f719298fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x\\le 2$ or $x\\ge -2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491722} {:answer/id #uuid "f6121d20-e884-5a07-9ef4-25a31648abf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\le x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491875}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491607} :answer-field/id #uuid "9deac3f0-5baf-51d6-9fda-87f6599c1dbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8a1c7bea-76d1-5380-abbc-23d155edc522" :question/math-academy-id "q-28797" :question/problem "![](images/67/67c1fa5db66f96b6bbbccacf251f3cdc655fc88a6b87499234f4b1cd0a21da1d.png)\n\nWhich inequality corresponds to the highlighted region shown on the number line above?" :question/worked-solution "The red segment contains all values between  $-2$ and $2.$\n\n- the closed circle at $-2$ implies that $x$ can be equal to $-2,$ while\n\n- the closed circle at $2$ implies that $x$ can be equal to $2.$\n\nTherefore, the highlighted region represents $-2\\le x\\le 2.$"} :step/id #uuid "5ec340ac-62fa-5a0a-99de-ca8554313a1d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492079}}] :activity/title "Compound Inequalities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491445}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111763} :activity/id #uuid "cceda887-69b2-551b-ab27-e3d019c77f5a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049068 :topic/id #uuid "d78ca6bb-945f-51db-a4af-fc777024df22" :topic/math-academy-id 1459} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111900 :tutorial/content "We can organize all mentioned types of quadrilaterals into the following hierarchy diagram:\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nEach shape below another is a specific type of the one above it. For example, every square is a rectangle, and every rectangle is a parallelogram. This means every square is also a parallelogram." :tutorial/id #uuid "d9d3be2f-753b-5a4b-9f26-20b34ab8f89a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5931 :tutorial/title "Classifying Quadrilaterals"} :step/id #uuid "eab37537-55a4-56e5-9fd6-7ee823f295bb" :step/math-academy-id 36207 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111765}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111845 :tutorial/content "There are lots of different types of quadrilaterals. Let's look at some examples.\n\n- A **trapezoid** is a quadrilateral with exactly ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) pair of opposite sides that are parallel to each other. \nThe parallel sides are called the **bases**. The other two sides are called the **legs**.\n\n![](images/9f/9fa09c46a2119d35c796065a696954931e20062fd5b3677a5484bef92bc2b0d8.png)\n\n- An **isosceles trapezoid** is a  trapezoid with congruent legs.\n\n![](images/b6/b6b2b2012c1d724f9da8b23551ec4f978f190d86875d7db7a3074635d7f40c02.png)\n\n- A **parallelogram** is a quadrilateral with ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of parallel sides.\n\n \n\n![](images/5d/5d0f383f3510f54b415a7ab99ef52fb30270924f97b8afec59534a96cd3edcef.png)\n\n Opposite sides and opposite angles of a parallelogram are always congruent. Moreover, if a quadrilateral has ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of opposite congruent sides or  ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of opposite congruent angles, it is always a parallelogram.\n\n![](images/39/39ccf26333785f9ef47eed68a96bc40005a6ba025fdb6e250beefe14f10b043d.png)\n\n- A **rectangle** is a parallelogram where all angles are right.\n\n \n\n![](images/55/55a16e0ba93bf1f01d576cca558805aaea9631d1fa8e43b149509d4f8eb4c834.png)\n\n A rectangle where all sides are congruent is a **square**.\n\n![](images/a0/a04b2a0e86be8b085c5e3fc906d5aa66dfea45c033d52cd4746031202a7d427d.png)\n\n- A **rhombus** is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. The opposite angles of a rhombus are also congruent.\n\n![](images/d3/d3588d7cbe8f285198df3909df3c03fdec04f53a3f75769e46a0ab4283fa27b3.png)\n\n A rhombus where all angles are congruent is a **square**.\n\n \n\n![](images/94/941872050d5407bcd9cb532e318276adccff802230ed854a2d292666fc0096fc.png)\n\n- A **kite** is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other.\n\n![](images/11/116e323d81ee190c0f69b4d7cdcefe9f0952da883b0d6d3c6d4036119794baa7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "06a11fa2-5911-52f5-9a6d-1028e49b44dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2262 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "04c2712b-f67a-5317-b34e-8936b6acdd9c" :step/math-academy-id 9452 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111598}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111765 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110405 :question/id #uuid "515ab620-8513-55cc-8c88-61c0ad27aff2" :question/math-academy-id "e-2833"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00889b75-3745-52f6-9157-912846482689" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049584}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446853}] :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Quadrilateral"} :step/id #uuid "4f398499-a037-5d65-8f25-ecbcfa7534c2" :step/math-academy-id 9454 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111767}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111767 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110777 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110406 :question/id #uuid "ee3a028c-93df-5e5a-bc06-4c286a5e1839" :question/math-academy-id "e-2834"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c81cf5ed-8dd7-5174-b8e9-faf63bf0a278" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531582}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadrilateral"} :step/id #uuid "14f9869d-2b87-58dd-a3bd-3dfd6047425b" :step/math-academy-id 9459 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111768}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111768 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110778 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110408 :question/id #uuid "a7b82dde-36fc-582e-9ed6-fbfcfbf8b47c" :question/math-academy-id "e-2839"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34ed7bcd-3c06-592e-906c-5010e80370db" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506571}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Quadrilaterals"} :step/id #uuid "615b803e-ca7c-56d1-9167-9829ed84b5c5" :step/math-academy-id 9460}] :activity/title "Classifying Quadrilaterals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110831}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506608} :activity/id #uuid "0fada08e-9ee8-50a7-a281-27eecc4451e2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049068 :topic/id #uuid "d78ca6bb-945f-51db-a4af-fc777024df22" :topic/math-academy-id 1459} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04cd6f91-4830-5fb2-a760-6d1458397aff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505925} {:answer/id #uuid "3cb14bc8-1566-5a66-a92e-6847a87ef3a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506011} {:answer/id #uuid "40c8a717-049b-5524-b555-5e7634dfb774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506019} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6d04c0-fedf-5f99-93ce-81d660f42b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506097} {:answer/id #uuid "9f2a5be0-7fd3-5d96-a0c9-2797fdcde248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506011} :answer-field/id #uuid "b73d6aaf-8b71-5986-9a72-f3f8cba064c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "16005fe9-c0e8-5807-8dbf-172a83d89558" :question/math-academy-id "q-53062" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. A square is sometimes not a rectangle.\n\nII. A rectangle is always a quadrilateral.\n\nIII. A square is never a rhombus." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. In a rectangle, its four angles are right angles. The same is true for a square. A square is a specific type of rectangle, where the four angles are right angles and the four sides are congruent.\n\n- Statement II is true. A rectangle is always a quadrilateral because it has four sides.\n\n- Statement III is false. A rhombus is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. A square is a specific type of rhombus, where the four angles are right angles and the four sides are congruent.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."} :step/id #uuid "1472f937-314f-5429-8263-e1a67f2a5b4d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506644}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "015f0057-b143-57ad-8fa5-2978f72ea3dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505914} {:answer/id #uuid "1afb074a-342e-57b7-b473-fffc06924988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505957} {:answer/id #uuid "555289bb-109d-55d5-955e-bed2ff4c8443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506057} {:answer/id #uuid "92f03582-9a2a-59ce-8ac4-d6529d374b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506160} {:answer/id #uuid "e905ed52-46a6-5b4b-9ca4-e3a4bea9ebdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506337}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506160} :answer-field/id #uuid "38a2df37-5f07-59b3-9c74-e390691ea42b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "978998ae-c2d1-5709-a415-cc49252cea0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-53063" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. A parallelogram is sometimes a rectangle.\n\nII. A kite is never a quadrilateral.\n\nIII. A square is always a trapezoid." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. A parallelogram is a rectangle when all the angles are right.\n\n- Statement II is false. A kite is always a quadrilateral because it has four sides.\n\n- Statement III is false. A trapezoid has only $1$ pair of opposite sides that are parallel. However, in a square, $2$ pairs of opposite sides are parallel, and so doesn't fit the definition of a trapezoid.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."} :step/id #uuid "a7b19881-e4d4-5ce2-a2db-ec80491290b8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506648}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c5ff65a-e0ad-54f7-95cd-72299fae692b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506101} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8a2580-e988-59db-b88f-bdf851f5dff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506224} {:answer/id #uuid "dabfe65d-e001-5f6f-8eae-84c4f049a38c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506304} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b01984-7aec-5b99-b309-32e1700f88cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506364} {:answer/id #uuid "faa76e22-ab2a-5e2f-b51a-88f71d4a4169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506371}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506364} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c39ae96-03b7-5d75-924b-50c66d2fd7a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4ee12482-99b3-5022-8d03-b6e6abfce44f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74591" :question/problem "![](images/e3/e3708d7723606224e201147b432250dd759022fd7ed275dabebf51935d68d8f8.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a rhombus?" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a rhombus is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is a rectangle. The sides are not all congruent, so it is not a rhombus.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a square. All its sides are congruent and the opposite sides are parallel, so it is a rhombus.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a trapezoid. Since it has only one pair of parallel sides, it is not a rhombus.\n\n- The shape $S$ is an isosceles trapezoid.  Since it has only one pair of parallel sides, it is not a rhombus.\n\nTherefore, shape $Q$ is a rhombus. The shapes $P,$ $R,$ and $S$ are not rhombuses."} :step/id #uuid "b98f15bb-0b73-5666-9f9e-51644b2b10be"}] :activity/title "Classifying Quadrilaterals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505900}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097538} :activity/id #uuid "1b2432bc-44f8-5039-b360-0a329be037b5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049355 :topic/id #uuid "ee459d83-61aa-5856-8bac-7a5980791833" :topic/math-academy-id 1131} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096676 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096463 :question/id #uuid "0d40f932-5646-5b71-b427-0b9477784939" :question/math-academy-id "e-4760"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67067a80-2143-5532-b6e2-bbbf59d862c6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048911}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446310}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting a Positive Number From a Smaller Positive Number"} :step/id #uuid "25292abb-7900-52be-874a-4cb2d06d6261" :step/math-academy-id 17350 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097527}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097527 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096677 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096464 :question/id #uuid "f6286c4b-0268-5430-88f4-f6185180f010" :question/math-academy-id "e-4761"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c37cc3b9-6255-5fb6-bb61-6cae10cd9531" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048546}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531647}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding a Positive Number to a Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "4cf6efa7-fe9e-53d9-a769-b858d4c73d9f" :step/math-academy-id 17351 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097528}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097528 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096678 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096465 :question/id #uuid "856921bd-1130-5b5f-8979-55ff188ac9ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-4763"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ff8566a-bc65-50a8-bd72-354631883152" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049626}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531559}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting a Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "b2f9b640-68f3-53a2-8b75-4c6d5d6dd4a5" :step/math-academy-id 17353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097529}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097529 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096679 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096466 :question/id #uuid "a596d16e-8e1b-5c20-8d82-b25e5b746955" :question/math-academy-id "e-4764"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c9e3ef7-2d6f-526e-9ceb-fb21040b639e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531630}] :knowledge-point/title "Combining Operations on Negative Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "ba0cdcf3-57a5-532a-a328-3b1526e640a6" :step/math-academy-id 17354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097538 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097845 :tutorial/content "We can solve some real-world problems using addition and subtraction of real numbers.\n\n- We use addition when there is an increase in the data.\n\n- We use subtraction to find a difference, or when there is a decrease in the data.\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "fb231454-f74e-5ee3-a4b4-f0ede88e0eca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3263 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5d30e33d-032b-5a2f-8701-d926f3d4c70e" :step/math-academy-id 17961 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097526}}] :activity/title "Solving Problems Using Addition and Subtraction of Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096903}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501297} :activity/id #uuid "6717b18e-0ae6-5129-8fd0-6d170c347d50" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049355 :topic/id #uuid "ee459d83-61aa-5856-8bac-7a5980791833" :topic/math-academy-id 1131} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501281 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097334 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43af25d8-92f4-5878-a3dc-1e644c8ce222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{4.1}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500598} {:answer/id #uuid "5af32836-d1a7-5d0a-943b-b796a712c9a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{5.4}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500653} {:answer/id #uuid "89cd1393-5088-5478-9c9a-365dbf9b98ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{4.5}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500756} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e8a032-002e-52f4-b130-3642ea6a8d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{4.9}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500816} {:answer/id #uuid "aee93dc4-7161-5dc3-8fb5-fae906b155a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{3.8}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500833}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500653} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e8d554f-b2db-5405-9a00-6a130d18be15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8ee7811c-bbf0-5938-a2b2-9a34707608bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-68929" :question/problem "Jennifer is doing a science experiment in her Chemistry class. She takes a liquid with a temperature of ${10.1}^{∘}\\text{C}$ and places it in a refrigerator. After half an hour, she removes the liquid from the refrigerator and places it on a table. She concludes that the temperature decreased by ${12.7}^{∘}\\text{C}.$\n\nAfter half an hour, Jennifer takes the temperature of the liquid again and finds that the temperature increased by ${8}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ What is the final temperature of the liquid?" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we must determine the temperature of the liquid when it was removed from the refrigerator. Since its temperature decreased by ${12.7}^{∘}\\text{C}$ compared to the initial temperature, we must subtract the variation in temperature from the initial temperature. This gives\n\n$$\n{10.1}^{∘}\\text{C}-{12.7}^{∘}\\text{C}=-{2.6}^{∘}\\text{C}.\n$$\n\nNow, we must determine the temperature of the liquid after half an hour had elapsed. Since the temperature of the liquid increased by ${8}^{∘}\\text{C}$ compared to the temperature when it was removed from the refrigerator, we add the variation in temperature to the temperature of the liquid when it was removed from the refrigerator. This gives\n\n$$\n-{2.6}^{∘}\\text{C}+{8}^{∘}\\text{C}={5.4}^{∘}\\text{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final temperature of the liquid is ${5.4}^{∘}\\text{C}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b210e935-1e45-56a0-b060-d68f4c88d265"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501285 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b50f720-834e-538d-a417-e00fac3153c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{1.2}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500555} {:answer/id #uuid "51dca9e2-de80-5c9d-8a95-e67f670877be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{1.3}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500635} {:answer/id #uuid "775a09e2-42f5-548f-8dd4-b32b4dc2ffac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{1.7}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500714} {:answer/id #uuid "a5596099-ee8b-5cf9-8adb-ce7fa5b6cd6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{2.3}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500805} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d3652a-d1a5-5019-9d30-94746544d406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{2.1}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500948}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500714} :answer-field/id #uuid "03913dbe-158c-5e69-a492-f412ab726635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0f9448c2-f63c-5cfa-ac1b-26b1b5d3b6f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-68889" :question/problem "On a certain day in January, the temperature in New York in the morning was $-{2.4}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$ At noon, the temperature registered a slight increase of ${0.7}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$ What was the temperature in New York at noon?" :question/worked-solution "To determine the temperature at noon, we add the variation in temperature to the temperature in the morning of that day This gives\n\n$$\n-{2.4}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}+{0.7}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}=-{1.7}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature in New York at noon that day was $-{1.7}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b66eda3a-f894-548a-a50a-1339b390ccd6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501281}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43d5adc5-a2eb-531a-b7ea-b5939b327f6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500599} {:answer/id #uuid "86933486-a8ce-5022-91e0-69867dfc556a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500748} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f2cce9-f1d9-5104-a5a1-b0915a465a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500794} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2d2cd5-3aa6-5d90-8deb-5b7155be97f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500856} {:answer/id #uuid "e5cee887-50b6-56ef-81f1-ae790574664e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500794} :answer-field/id #uuid "813aaeae-688c-5e88-936e-02dac71e1499" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "beabbd10-476d-53ed-8111-c1ff6cabe7de" :question/math-academy-id "q-68474" :question/problem "Margaret and Juan like freediving. One day they both went to the sea, and while Juan practiced this extreme sport $13$ meters below sea level, Margaret waited for him in the boat, $2$ meters above sea level. If Juan is below Margaret, how far apart are they from each other?" :question/worked-solution "To determine the distance between Margaret and Juan, we calculate the difference between Margaret's location and Juan's location. This gives\n\n$$\n2-(-13)=2+13=15\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Margaret and Juan are $15\\,\\text{m}$ apart."} :step/id #uuid "f6f16938-eb50-51e6-8e2a-41104a49a9ed" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501285}}] :activity/title "Solving Problems Using Addition and Subtraction of Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500463}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531684} :activity/id #uuid "3a7cc79b-02e8-5c46-8409-33face8b5d86" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049355 :topic/id #uuid "ee459d83-61aa-5856-8bac-7a5980791833" :topic/math-academy-id 1131} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531647 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "522a5cb8-0595-52ba-9fc4-f068d4c71551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{6.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531044} {:answer/id #uuid "9f446274-2a06-55e0-8045-82df46c8c668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{5.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531210} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2b2c82-d14e-5b84-a678-29528d106c3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{5.1}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531265} {:answer/id #uuid "cecbd7c4-4b6e-5c63-af83-fbf23e812e6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{5.7}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531293} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb4664a-819a-5fb1-8000-71d73f25fc83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{6.1}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531382}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531382} :answer-field/id #uuid "36172dc8-5c18-5ef5-9115-2e2f3e7fffef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "de213b3e-d398-5228-8036-33cbe3a6bb3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-68888" :question/problem "Jordan did an experiment in his Chemistry class. He heated a liquid whose initial temperature was $-{1.2}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$ After several minutes, the temperature of the liquid increased by ${7.3}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}$, at which point he decided to end the experiment. What was the final temperature of the liquid?" :question/worked-solution "To determine the final temperature of the liquid, we add the variation in temperature to the initial temperature of the liquid. This gives\n\n$$\n-{1.2}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}+{7.3}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}={6.1}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature of the liquid at the end of the experiment was ${6.1}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$"} :step/id #uuid "4312b0ab-5d64-50e6-af89-0104161e7489" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531721}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531557 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18077ca4-3183-5672-8449-0b9577e66287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{20.0}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530910} {:answer/id #uuid "27359f95-680c-5018-a7ac-5a2779c5560b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{19.4}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530953} {:answer/id #uuid "6703364e-cb34-54b1-bbee-575941b2ee4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{19.0}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531093} {:answer/id #uuid "74447b91-7282-514f-97dd-a72921fec8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{18.0}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531121} {:answer/id #uuid "7eaacb5e-d719-5348-ada6-db919d5cf4fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{18.4}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531141}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530953} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8439ccf-f19f-54b5-951f-b6eba6db8136" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "02fcb6dd-d8d1-548e-be72-9006c65abdf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-72691" :question/problem "Chantelle did an experiment in her Chemistry class. She heated a liquid whose initial temperature was $-{5.3}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$ After several minutes, the temperature of the liquid increased by ${24.7}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}$, at which point she decided to end the experiment. What was the final temperature of the liquid?" :question/worked-solution "To determine the final temperature of the liquid, we add the variation in temperature to the initial temperature of the liquid. This gives\n\n$$\n-{5.3}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}+{24.7}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}={19.4}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature of the liquid at the end of the experiment was ${19.4}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b3d2d012-a306-590e-b68d-a518598a0966"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531721 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531630 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0233853c-7d57-5992-a3de-9375ae31e3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{2.9}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530866} {:answer/id #uuid "11599972-180e-564a-8500-e2e61a9c6541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{3.7}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530894} {:answer/id #uuid "11d8cfac-231b-5b5e-a776-de1a254087fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{5.3}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530897} {:answer/id #uuid "565ce699-4384-5be5-a535-10eb3fabc2a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{5.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531053} {:answer/id #uuid "64fa1bca-1096-5716-a7aa-e09d16681a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{3.4}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531087}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530894} :answer-field/id #uuid "89a90e23-11a2-5c55-80c6-c90a2ec355b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aab3a1fe-f42d-5861-9d8c-8da472abefec" :question/math-academy-id "q-68925" :question/problem "In Toronto, Canada, the average temperature in November is ${4.9}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$ In December, the average temperature decreases by ${5.4}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}$ compared to November. Finally, in January, the average temperature decreases by ${3.2}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}$ compared to the previous month. Determine the average temperature in Toronto in January." :question/worked-solution "First, we must determine the average temperature in Toronto during the month of December. Since we know that the temperature decreases on average by ${5.4}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}$ compared to November, we subtract the variation in temperature from the average temperature in November. This gives\n\n$$\n{4.9}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}-{5.4}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}=-{0.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.\n$$\n\nNow, we must determine the temperature during the month of January. Since we know that the temperature decreases on average by ${3.2}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}$ compared to December, we subtract the variation in temperature from the average temperature in December. This gives\n\n$$\n-{0.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}-{3.2}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}=-{3.7}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average temperature in Toronto in January is $-{3.7}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$"} :step/id #uuid "ddd3f114-58f8-562c-960c-b5c342a5bcb5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531707}}] :activity/title "Solving Problems Using Addition and Subtraction of Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530855}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074775} :activity/id #uuid "e7d0e915-5cc0-5d0f-b4c6-027cb6220aa3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049463 :topic/id #uuid "f762d717-d0f3-5d60-8a4d-561cc023f344" :topic/math-academy-id 524} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074775 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075029 :tutorial/content "Given a number, its **additive inverse** is the number we need to add to get zero.\nTo find the additive inverse of a number, we change its sign by placing a minus symbol $({\\color{red}{-}})$ in front of it. \n\nFor example, to find the additive inverse of $5,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{-}}\\,(5) = -5.\n$$\n\nTo check our work, we add the inverse to the original number. If we're correct, we should get zero.\n\n$$\n5+(-5) = 5-5 = 0 \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "21fd73d9-f9b8-5ef8-9e18-9ba21d6d8d79" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15159 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c1f41837-dee8-5f07-9c9f-5cfaebd69d2e" :step/math-academy-id 620103 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074776}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074776 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073820 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073437 :question/id #uuid "735e0a05-bc90-5b18-b096-d51d9f9c50e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-22709"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7d26bc9-6f89-5da4-b8a9-6be6f4280f81" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050421}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445912}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Positive Integer"} :step/id #uuid "6255e96d-283e-52d1-815a-3ba256caf351" :step/math-academy-id 620104 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074777}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074777 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075030 :tutorial/content "To find the additive inverse of a negative number, we can use the same process.\n\nFor instance, to find the additive inverse of the number $-3,$ we change its sign by placing a minus symbol $({\\color{red}{-}})$ in front of it.\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{-}}\\,(-3) = 3.\n$$\n\nAgain, we check our work by adding the inverse to the original number. If we're correct, we should get zero.\n\n$$\n(-3)+3 = -3+3 = 0 \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d30313d5-d29e-5eea-b11c-0619439becb0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15161 :tutorial/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Negative Integer"} :step/id #uuid "29da515a-cf3c-5833-83f4-fdcc7d1ce495" :step/math-academy-id 620107 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074778}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074778 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073821 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073438 :question/id #uuid "de14e5df-9b12-5878-a134-9b02db1d5b5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-22710"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b38f30b0-9e05-510f-9ba6-928ab1b8482b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048546}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531586}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Negative Integer"} :step/id #uuid "d90caf33-37bf-5639-9bbb-33de68320604" :step/math-academy-id 620108 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074779}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074779 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073822 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073439 :question/id #uuid "8b152054-aec8-58f3-9a7a-e435e49fe8c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-22711"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b88b2def-7312-5076-9ff3-60319d37f1d6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445908}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Decimal or Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "0a3e5261-c30f-51be-aebe-8a0790994019" :step/math-academy-id 620111}] :activity/title "Additive Inverses of Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073944}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148561} :activity/id #uuid "ce8303ff-2050-5cb3-8c68-076feb3951ea" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049467 :topic/id #uuid "f7d14b60-9964-50e8-9416-a68a8f8500eb" :topic/math-academy-id 2454} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148699 :tutorial/content "Percentages are *very* common in everyday life. For example, they are used in retail, finance, and sports! In this lesson, we will learn how to calculate the percentage of a whole number in real-world situations.\n\nFor example, suppose Daniel and Luisa traveled $200$ kilometers in their car. If Luisa drove $40 \\%$ of the way, how many kilometers did she drive?\n\nTo solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = \\textrm{percentage} \\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the whole is the number of kilometers traveled, which is $200$ kilometers. The percentage is $40 \\%,$ and the part is the distance Luisa drove.\n\nAs usual, we can use two methods to solve this problem:\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal: \n\n$$\n40 \\% = 40 \\div 100  = 0.4\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =40%\\times 200 \\\\  & =0.4\\times 200 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo Luisa drove $80$ kilometers.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n40 \\% = \\dfrac{40}{100} = \\dfrac{40 \\div 20}{100 \\div 20} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =40%\\times 200 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\times 200 \\\\  & =\\frac{400}{5} \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we find that Luisa drove $80$ kilometers." :tutorial/id #uuid "b32fe964-00bc-58b8-8e13-0ebde913bd87" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2080 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6f30bc9e-6f94-5aa2-a2a2-62ed9c078df2" :step/math-academy-id 7956 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147593 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147248 :question/id #uuid "f888277f-085b-5c6a-b50c-8ffd9095bc53" :question/math-academy-id "e-2327"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3626bdd2-ec13-5d74-a570-ec65ba682723" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050716}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442103}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage"} :step/id #uuid "6d044eff-fad0-5933-9034-a4181e67deeb" :step/math-academy-id 7957 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147594 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147249 :question/id #uuid "03c003db-96d2-5588-a1fa-944b9305cf6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-2329"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "163d387b-3fd7-5f95-8245-5fb0184b371b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531631}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Decimal Percentage"} :step/id #uuid "61efc867-f64d-544b-ba1c-5cfc49f6333b" :step/math-academy-id 7959 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147595 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147250 :question/id #uuid "f409e34f-c8f7-5e21-b024-a0c7012c496d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2330"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "176069e4-4aa3-58cd-a04e-a70e7e5da960" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048806}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527137}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage Greater Than 100%"} :step/id #uuid "55dcb5aa-7569-519c-a7c0-b114e3d83f67" :step/math-academy-id 7960}] :activity/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage: Word Problems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147655}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491237} :activity/id #uuid "9ea2bef4-3b98-5591-a696-0b49d8520af2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049467 :topic/id #uuid "f7d14b60-9964-50e8-9416-a68a8f8500eb" :topic/math-academy-id 2454} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b445a131-81f0-5514-a192-d5eb422bef26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$112$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144975} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e8430f-d1aa-5170-bb82-785391bf344f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$128$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144976} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a296a4-550e-588e-b32e-e9e4afbfb734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$117$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144977} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b6ecbc-2867-5b7a-a528-e7f2ca3a3967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$121$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144978} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0261e5-a932-5e3f-8956-c8d434bf15ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$130$ kilograms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144979}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144977} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0247569-e442-53e7-b321-6e1aaaeee8a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d7988730-acb0-5c20-b331-3dbab826b8f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-61174" :question/problem "A concrete mix weighs $600$ kilograms. Of this amount, $19.5%$ represents the weight of the crushed stone in the mix. How much crushed stone is there in the concrete mix?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the weight of the concrete mix, which is $600$ kilograms. The percentage is $19.5%,$ and the part is the weight of the crushed stone in the mix.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n19.5%=19.5÷100=0.195\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =19.5%\\times 600 \\\\  & =0.195\\times 600 \\\\  & =117\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $19.5%$ of $600$ is $117.$ Thus, there are $117$ kilograms of crushed stone in the concrete mix.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n19.5%=\\frac{19.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =19.5%\\times 600 \\\\  & =\\frac{19.5}{100}\\times 600 \\\\  & =19.5\\times \\frac{600}{100} \\\\  & =19.5\\times 6 \\\\  & =117\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $19.5%$ of $600$ is $117.$ Thus, there are $117$ kilograms of crushed stone in the concrete mix."} :step/id #uuid "514173b4-add8-5a8b-884e-61c59887c63f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491267}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491241 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3fed532-6ecc-54f6-bad0-b6ad07df33aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$21$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144987} {:answer/id #uuid "2fbe64f4-a22c-5777-a3ec-19b32abc5173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$22$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144988} {:answer/id #uuid "33f5d0f0-b1cd-5f13-9fb4-4005ac8072ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$24$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144989} {:answer/id #uuid "767643b6-d9d5-5d8b-94f6-d58fe32ca1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$18$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144990} {:answer/id #uuid "7770f937-95a0-55a1-bb09-4a74b4daacc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$28$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144991}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144989} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b14e3b7-0f48-5b1e-a3df-3a11bc0b2ed6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "290a6299-631d-5270-a788-c595be029595" :question/math-academy-id "q-61188" :question/problem "Anna, a dressmaker, bought $15$ meters of fabric in a particular month to make children's pants. The following month, she decided to buy $160%$ of the amount of fabric she bought the previous month. How much fabric did Anna buy on her second purchase?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the amount of fabric Anna bought the first time, which is $15$ meters. The percentage is $160%,$ and the part is the amount of fabric she bought on the second purchase.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n160%=160÷100=1.6\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =160%\\times 15 \\\\  & =1.6\\times 15 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $160%$ of $15$ is $24.$ So, Anna bought $24$ meters of fabric on the second purchase.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n160%=\\frac{160}{100}=\\frac{160÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =160%\\times 15 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\times 15 \\\\  & =8\\times \\frac{15}{5} \\\\  & =8\\times 3 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $160%$ of $15$ is $24.$ So, Anna bought $24$ meters of fabric on the second purchase."} :step/id #uuid "64ed2f2c-78f8-59b1-af54-b0439b650035"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491267 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29b3a452-1742-57c1-9f1e-bc77b9df5dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "420" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490589} {:answer/id #uuid "61ce839e-834f-54a5-9c9e-f00fd0753c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490693} {:answer/id #uuid "744129d7-d0ce-5c8c-98a9-76b25bb784a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490739} {:answer/id #uuid "b833ff6e-69ba-52a2-81d4-7ba41f29f665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "430" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490876} {:answer/id #uuid "e1bd0ebf-e3a4-517c-90b3-10048e44189a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "380" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490938}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490693} :answer-field/id #uuid "f229c353-7ba4-5955-b6b1-8b5659543db4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c38eb0c5-c781-5447-a06b-aea59b9ebae6" :question/math-academy-id "q-61182" :question/problem "On the opening day of the children's baseball season, $300$ people attended the baseball game. The next game was attended by $120%$ of the number of people who attended the opening game. How many people attended the second game?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of people who attended the opening game, which is $300$ people. The percentage is $120%,$ and the part is the number of people who attended the second game.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n120%=120÷100=1.2\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 300 \\\\  & =1.2\\times 300 \\\\  & =360\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $300$ is $360.$ So, $360$ people attended the second game.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n120%=\\frac{120}{100}=\\frac{120÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =120%\\times 300 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\times 300 \\\\  & =6\\times \\frac{300}{5} \\\\  & =6\\times 60 \\\\  & =360\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $120%$ of $300$ is $360.$ So, $360$ people attended the second game."} :step/id #uuid "c868de51-fc0a-5c14-a54e-be88e0c2566f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491241}}] :activity/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage: Word Problems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490506}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074901} :activity/id #uuid "71a6f31c-0bb8-5061-8779-9e3b7984d66e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049868 :topic/id #uuid "19c222a3-03e5-5eb1-b6c2-e026705f9eda" :topic/math-academy-id 573} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074898 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073898 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073587 :question/id #uuid "fb5d2269-5350-5d79-9414-1a25caa48d8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-2647"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d9b6948-92f4-58b3-8c06-fae61765dc57" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050517}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441871}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Horizontal Distance Between Two Points"} :step/id #uuid "123d2929-5563-5b7e-8588-e7734bb4eb42" :step/math-academy-id 8890 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074899}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073899 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073588 :question/id #uuid "1250785a-1ae3-5218-925a-0eac01281f3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-2648"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0708cd3-0608-5d57-ac14-4c16ef362473" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527138}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vertical Distance Between Two Points"} :step/id #uuid "f954535e-e2c6-5a01-85b8-f90fdccafc1b" :step/math-academy-id 8891 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073900 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073589 :question/id #uuid "e05457af-60c5-519f-935e-db465fc76efa" :question/math-academy-id "e-2649"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83bbdad9-c2a5-5caa-a4e9-2a0c904c605b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531593}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between Two Points: Crossing the Axis"} :step/id #uuid "3a335606-3bdb-5535-8956-9a3ababd7eb1" :step/math-academy-id 8892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074978 :tutorial/content "The distance between two points in the coordinate plane is the number of units between them. Let's explore two methods for calculating the horizontal distance between two points with the points $(1,1)$ and $(5,1).$\n\n**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFirst, we plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/19/196c50295e8d8a95084c31226fb9319ede7b70603d894b6939e4615df583c29c.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4.$\n\n![](images/4b/4b531e29616f3e8d7ea0c65761e59cfea224b9c0e756c68b7647c7271dc09493.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/6d/6d5c6760e15098dc381e639cd806b6410f2fa6d9049d11d5467427635c9872b6.png)\n\nThe point $({\\color{blue}{1}},1)$ is at a distance of $\\color{blue}{1}$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $({\\color{red}{5}},1)$ is at a distance of $\\color{red}{5}$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is ${\\color{red}{5}} - {\\color{blue}{1}} = 4.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "aeee8d0d-20f6-5700-8c35-c66e0eef8daa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2199 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1961bdcc-cea7-5d78-88c1-4f69e1dab833" :step/math-academy-id 8895 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074898}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074979 :tutorial/content "When we want to find the distance between two points in different quadrants, method 1 works exactly the same, but method 2 is slightly different.\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis for a horizontal distance, or $x$-axis for a vertical distance, and then *adding*. \n\nSo, what's the distance between $(-3,2)$ and $(1,2)?$\n\n**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/e0/e0fec8fca43b421b62efc3d6d892563c80beaab3c2816be47aa7563868555268.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4.$\n\n![](images/dd/ddc1e6cec323fe245ff2a115a587e773d408dc0115ba5002c02ac1d238620c63.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](images/0b/0bbb91fc8c0e7ad577261dcdbc8e7aaa16a44e8be2e937e2d6870aae58af4ccf.png)\n\nThe point $({\\color{blue}{1}},2)$ is at a distance of $\\color{blue}{1}$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $({\\color{red}{-3}},2)$ is at a distance of $\\color{red}{3}$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is ${\\color{red}{3}} + {\\color{blue}{1}} = 4.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a1233efd-87e1-5707-9b70-24c24ed47418" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2204 :tutorial/title "The Distance Between Two Points in Different Quadrants"} :step/id #uuid "d97c5e30-e125-5cba-9973-1e1898eca20e" :step/math-academy-id 8947 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074900}}] :activity/title "Distances Between Points in the Coordinate Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073991}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495267} :activity/id #uuid "4ad6406f-28ca-5b57-94e6-4b4cc6fef04e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049868 :topic/id #uuid "19c222a3-03e5-5eb1-b6c2-e026705f9eda" :topic/math-academy-id 573} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bdc2701-e79a-5b33-86a6-ef27722a55d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494506} {:answer/id #uuid "6822bf50-caeb-554f-938f-a5cc8683af71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494526} {:answer/id #uuid "80300fbe-d90a-591a-928d-9196db5a2240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494596} {:answer/id #uuid "b85c490c-92e9-5030-a0f6-291132b15fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494748} {:answer/id #uuid "dceb000c-8083-5f83-9e97-ae002b6b6ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494841}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494506} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a673c29-4fae-58d2-bcf6-86accdee64d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "95857c13-ed71-5f23-a1a7-f058676d3273" :question/math-academy-id "q-161670" :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $(-1,5)$  and $(3,5)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/74/74dfce8c8444a841f9f742d70204d82b22157bf5b955bb2691eb0cdefe62fb75.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4.$\n\n![](images/4d/4df0d2515a43236634f934879d6a4d8926cc09163f79216ce288e07e2ba28e72.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](images/aa/aaf091301a2b004e80bef49a2893eed00f0a28693d8799ee366ed47425869a9c.png)\n\nThe point $(-1,5)$ is at a distance of $1$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $(3,5)$ is at a distance of $3$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $1+3=4.$"} :step/id #uuid "a89caa7f-1651-5452-aad2-2238b199389e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495257 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42efa052-c9ab-54d8-9f6d-f34afac5ba39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494453} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec87af5-64e1-5fd2-bd75-166d861e724a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494553} {:answer/id #uuid "70a17d06-9360-5c19-9fa3-a5b4fb34974f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494559} {:answer/id #uuid "c731f4ef-fe9c-53df-889c-769685b74d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494787} {:answer/id #uuid "ea8a321d-e3fc-594f-880d-2f2880f86d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494553} :answer-field/id #uuid "7857e68d-f576-5073-be69-923b7ef4e7c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fce25e13-2247-5592-b858-9038960a704e" :question/math-academy-id "q-61243" :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $(-5,-2)$  and $(-5,-9)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/35/353bedad7a1692dea35f8b6adf30b7b461458c7852b4123a3fc865e1b23370a6.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $7$ units, so the distance equals $7.$\n\n![](images/e3/e3516729d7662a3b4438a3d3085da3428605d026ba3387a71afeb085e2bd3e73.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/f8/f8c5583d14d4e5ca50df3ce906254446c402177734f352169d794de2bb7dea5c.png)\n\nThe point $(-5,-2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-5,-9)$ is at a distance of $9$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $9-2=7.$"} :step/id #uuid "d5842dc7-b1b5-522c-8151-6d2e56afbed8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495237}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495267 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495146 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4844ffa7-9148-54b8-9e72-b30c1cb0c11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494463} {:answer/id #uuid "800b7dfe-1222-53bf-82a3-9afc3077f999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494595} {:answer/id #uuid "b32d799a-1971-505f-9d44-6d88dea78839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494736} {:answer/id #uuid "cde81723-b01c-5413-aefe-9f6e7a2f5589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494806} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3d1b5c-cd0f-5511-8f83-2c24cef83452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494736} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce35d819-2898-51ad-bcff-556e53b6cc07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d345ec95-0d6e-5d8e-a681-8aa64a38a770" :question/math-academy-id "q-61238" :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $(-2,2)$  and $(-2,7)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/39/39ac0f7718d7ee4ca8ed96b43c4f5ff9b644e0a3f7b5e2629052468b36ccf687.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $5$ units, so the distance equals $5.$\n\n![](images/59/5995f7494a28a33cc4bdeef51d7d4cc0a85ee8e3219d8630d366c50345629d54.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/1c/1cb541f6fcbb93b30a8f154763b0daa60e3b76142db1001ce804a2a68ba79247.png)\n\nThe point $(-2,7)$ is at a distance of $7$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-2,2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $7-2=5.$"} :step/id #uuid "ef3e2ccc-1312-5209-8ec4-fde9203f2c0d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495257}}] :activity/title "Distances Between Points in the Coordinate Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494305}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527232} :activity/id #uuid "6400b63d-ece5-55c3-ace8-f3cf09eb034e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049868 :topic/id #uuid "19c222a3-03e5-5eb1-b6c2-e026705f9eda" :topic/math-academy-id 573} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527232 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02dbb7c8-1405-51e2-be89-1394776caad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526506} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc1dfda-0971-51d2-b2f6-fd8adfa0f1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526529} {:answer/id #uuid "24e265ed-ab68-54e6-95e4-cfa966d948c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526583} {:answer/id #uuid "cf7ea040-309d-5faf-bab9-81177579d7ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526900} {:answer/id #uuid "d456bc45-475c-5809-ba80-4e99f3a8ac38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526911}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526529} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c47ba7d-aa0c-5abb-a75b-b6921e6c2540" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0a27d18e-df05-520d-948c-41b1732464a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-60606" :question/problem "![](images/84/84da1d1bb056c8c5d823a585889699e3ba3fe5ca7b2706b4e0a16c96f2e65ac3.png)\n\nWhat is the distance between the points $(3,1)$  and $(3,4)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $3$ units, so the distance equals $3.$\n\n![](images/a0/a0eb36fd37d73ed20e0ee17717f80cb33a56910cc57eb181897ad44197a7f268.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/2b/2b4771270aec2dd62828f4063262cfaf81fba31f6be6d1a2ba8bfe0d59778b48.png)\n\nThe point $(3,4)$ is at a distance of $4$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(3,1)$ is at a distance of $1$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $4-1=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "12252c44-f6ed-5a4c-877a-529ad585fdea" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527255}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527243 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "068e3080-4337-581d-9b8b-cc0e663f7828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526516} {:answer/id #uuid "0a1c255d-9fb4-5411-b2aa-255dce8090e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526524} {:answer/id #uuid "155fbdce-2870-53b4-bdf7-758d24346ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526545} {:answer/id #uuid "21bbce83-c3c6-5f3b-ac9d-a19684c5d630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526575} {:answer/id #uuid "b70f45a0-ba84-55de-ab9d-38674206db65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526855}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526516} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba2c0b6c-0868-582f-9fb0-8f7239a4a7d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3ab19b4a-4627-54ee-8e9e-8d051f6ac141" :question/math-academy-id "q-61265" :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $(-3,2)$  and $(-3,-4)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/c0/c0dcef8b6c0c63e17fc9b516e3e3176f054dd0eddb93c71375a3de3272da9623.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $6$ units, so the distance equals $6.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1764499f4600d5c74c598b205e0a1971ca91231c4464f77e0809a680003bf11.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](images/5b/5b4c4c665b07575604dc873703517548404d7c31e8395070642ca37584c6d08f.png)\n\nThe point $(-3,2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-3,-4)$ is at a distance of $4$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $4+2=6.$"} :step/id #uuid "35f258ab-51ea-5cc4-ab06-dadec569e07a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527255 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "199f5bd1-cf24-5ec5-87fb-e715dfad0524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526557} {:answer/id #uuid "4446140c-ebf8-5421-a61b-bfae02059671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526633} {:answer/id #uuid "7e700299-d0cc-5e63-95d1-97a04cb6ea9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526752} {:answer/id #uuid "921e2d09-f58f-50dc-91f3-e6324b399fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526789} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd79885-eb4b-5b18-8642-27db39ab655a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526808}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526557} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a86d0ab-34e6-5c19-97fc-00a0a1fc39ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "67699b33-3e46-5c7c-ad2b-2f6df6bd8eb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-61105" :question/problem "![](images/25/25e1891ce31fa7043723b2dcb4cd1e01c41dbd82ae70ef5024c64ed2d6deab33.png)\n\nWhat is the distance between the points $(-3,1)$  and $(-3,-5)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $6$ units, so the distance equals $6.$\n\n![](images/1f/1f3eeede9aa301cf6d64b48cc86f770fc86517ed51285da9bdc86e5a580b243f.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](images/a5/a504ca22769a377cd7e23e0b6a09595d00c8ad25733f3d1e6a716b52f262e139.png)\n\nThe point $(-3,-5)$ is at a distance of $5$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-3,1)$ is at a distance of $1$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $5+1=6.$"} :step/id #uuid "5c465ab7-ebb0-5979-8fd6-300e20ddd25b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527287}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527287 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff3933f1-5330-5a93-bd06-b1a968921e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073029} {:answer/id #uuid "038610b4-8855-5613-ad62-a56e0c0f24e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073030} {:answer/id #uuid "288f063e-1947-5a0f-8867-d300a95793d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073031} {:answer/id #uuid "3d64ff07-ead1-5334-9540-a844d96b19ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073032} {:answer/id #uuid "4eced26e-1801-553d-a9a9-d047aee0b82a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073033}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073031} :answer-field/id #uuid "723fead1-3866-508a-9080-2df0ed86113b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3943d606-fff6-5e9c-a446-2d4378743487" :question/math-academy-id "q-61224" :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $(4,2)$  and $(4,8)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/66/66c988b7fede0e97eae46388dd0e57aee2c49a3c185c9a888277da8fefc947ab.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $6$ units, so the distance equals $6.$\n\n![](images/63/63f70c20c918dae51ecddc7891a8cbee7c5749720521e62fe26e9bc4e33bb9e6.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/d3/d3693b8c3d07636143b2c77c3bd2645015885b77ec4e8ddffde2e121da0511bc.png)\n\nThe point $(4,8)$ is at a distance of $8$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(4,2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $8-2=6.$"} :step/id #uuid "f27b2fa2-b4cf-5259-af6d-f395c41888da" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527243}}] :activity/title "Distances Between Points in the Coordinate Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526490}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111613} :activity/id #uuid "dc520173-74e8-57e7-ad6f-9e7a82ef78e1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049892 :topic/id #uuid "1c2006c0-c4c4-5ff8-8fa2-7f9dd3693f49" :topic/math-academy-id 1500} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111906 :tutorial/content "For any two distinct points, there is always one (and only one) line that passes through them.\n\nFor example, the line passing through the points $P$ and $Q$ is $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ},$ while the line that passes through $A$ and $B$ is $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/8c/8c79a551e7ced71de157ea292baa3d7f38afaca4ea76ee467fc4add1b28ed33f.png)\n\nHowever, given three or more points, there is not always a line that passes through all of them.\n\nFor example, for the three points $F, G,$ and $H$ below, it is impossible to draw a single straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](images/30/30361c0bd49e571e317746f2d88848626da52e5ddbcda8fe9b6f496e4aff6b69.png)\n\nHowever, it is *sometimes* possible to draw a line through three (or more) points. In such cases, we say that the points are **collinear**.\n\nFor example, the points $M,$ $N,$ and $O$ shown below are collinear since they all lie on the same line.\n\n![](images/bf/bf3de55518c21de30718b2d9fee7032139c89aa3a4a963b8346b457ceafbeada.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "051ac95a-dc63-5270-a5fa-a498a36c8297" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6732 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2afa6da4-491a-5a31-8b50-09bd210fc929" :step/math-academy-id 40727 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110674 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110295 :question/id #uuid "a042ba66-3567-5b06-84e6-344ad0043bbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10748"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f67c3319-1153-51ed-a57a-f85c06c7e76c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527150}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Points on Intersecting Lines Are Collinear"} :step/id #uuid "08fe2b06-8140-5116-be8c-f9a047f0e538" :step/math-academy-id 40728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110675 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110296 :question/id #uuid "76902823-634d-5525-afc5-5f2a98cea481" :question/math-academy-id "e-10749"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef4e321-841e-5933-b5e4-ff7dab6c5275" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049350}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531657}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Collinear Points on a Grid"} :step/id #uuid "f4bbacca-99b9-57fe-b9c5-a2075ef9663b" :step/math-academy-id 40731 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111614}}] :activity/title "Collinear Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110859}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531680} :activity/id #uuid "1e277d26-3cd4-5f96-8fca-4798fc8680e1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049892 :topic/id #uuid "1c2006c0-c4c4-5ff8-8fa2-7f9dd3693f49" :topic/math-academy-id 1500} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fe09bc4-31c7-568f-be87-02c28b0907ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530929} {:answer/id #uuid "4160685f-7ffd-5ade-9845-5db543cb5598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531003} {:answer/id #uuid "88414189-b1f0-5218-8c18-851c2eff974f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531162} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e424dc-7e89-5bd6-b6e8-84d2d9dea85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531271} {:answer/id #uuid "ec66f155-3fe5-5781-8a8a-8f6d97fb5483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531341}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531341} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff504920-6a29-5379-af64-ee43cc431a4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f44be398-7e63-5657-97ac-ad7b78dba187" :question/math-academy-id "q-155051" :question/problem "![](images/01/01aba7550fb134b1f3a93276dc8f3b91794844ea21254c2c75a0702a443a60e3.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above shows three collinear points?" :question/worked-solution "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Diagram I shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/63/6310ff39996be529bc44e5058ef9710400f0c8089196a57e7f4eb541703e53ae.png)\n\n- Diagram II shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/63/6334d2bc1fda6390b6ec542afb8cc37d4f76322b98d41ca6bbb3af3c8d735c8f.png)\n\n- Diagram III shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/89/8939cff2d7cc8ca9a08c7be63517ffb900e49b769edc5c2454119f4da25904cc.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "2a5c9c16-0e21-58e2-96cf-f2e548e46cfb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb0faf0f-5618-54d9-89a7-80c77140df42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110090} {:answer/id #uuid "f86d63d7-74b3-530e-91d9-8ff31f761445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110091} {:answer/id #uuid "479e057f-e9e5-52df-ba3d-412259c39194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110092} {:answer/id #uuid "6af6be9b-f51c-5bcf-b37a-cca78ef06518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110093} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccc7e23-2f5e-5bc7-b82f-ad9500638c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110094}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110091} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfb9f9a8-0793-550e-8000-7cbb65bc3ee6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7d9ca7f7-8b8f-513d-9055-bfafe5f332c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-7484" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1fd1460fe24739d1c1bf1a7cad39da6775860b0764eaa4b41fffaae073e3a50.png)\n\nGiven the picture shown above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $E$, $G$ and $H$ are collinear\n\nII. $A$, $B$ and $G$ are **not** collinear\n\nIII. $B$, $F$ and $H$ are collinear" :question/worked-solution "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. There is a straight line that passes through these three points.\n\n![](images/fb/fb82255ac578efc90bb070dab669a19950387cab7d9011de4d5d0d94dd9de505.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. We can't draw a straight line that passes through these three points.\n\n![](images/2c/2c20ebe076b3e655b7709ccb337c68d4aa8505bfabdb5a8755e5a1410642d612.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. We can't draw a straight line that passes through these three points.\n\n![](images/98/9859ea0144923d69e37d64130910608f6fc3e7afe09d74af19a5402030e0d3cb.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "31152b20-4269-5712-98a1-7b73a2698d69" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531693}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531693 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23cd9f25-8435-55d5-b158-0660cc1531d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530942} {:answer/id #uuid "2caca10d-0d8d-59c0-ac12-d60468871006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530962} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3c3526-8bc0-510b-9c22-722374cff8f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531135} {:answer/id #uuid "d4bd80d9-e2bb-50d2-8715-080b48547162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531303} {:answer/id #uuid "f3622c66-9de1-5f33-ba19-0af4be6d1d74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531360}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531360} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c338420-c385-5c49-a1ce-a9b479af623d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "73e5dd6e-2c47-5055-afc2-9cc17f2d620e" :question/math-academy-id "q-155031" :question/problem "![](images/97/97faa2ca35485f76f012475a9ba2b6af7938cfc86728bddd560cf3da59fff197.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above shows three collinear points?" :question/worked-solution "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Diagram I shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/f5/f52592db0fee86e26318d6d2de9d7f685f1fb9f5d416628a04878857f1c54b54.png)\n\n- Diagram II shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](images/76/76f0da764caf0237a2d30d32d593886d8df6c348368fe889f7447be781b6faf1.png)\n\n- Diagram III shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](images/97/9778abd955cdc9b705800eba8f3d0d635af1c6a5fd5724583bdb32a943c6d44f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "765a5c07-dcf7-5bfa-9917-01071a277a0b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531676}}] :activity/title "Collinear Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530854}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153002} :activity/id #uuid "26b78e45-1997-563c-b456-d53ae655f749" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049935 :topic/id #uuid "1fcf3c5f-af9e-5e5e-b7aa-ff9b3a312835" :topic/math-academy-id 2558} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153465 :tutorial/content "The **natural numbers** are the positive whole numbers:\n${\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2},\\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots$\nWe also refer to them as the **counting numbers**.\n\nThere's some debate among mathematicians about whether zero should be considered a natural number. However, for our purposes, zero is *not* a natural number." :tutorial/id #uuid "ddcec145-4774-553b-836d-76a4a5cd221e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9069 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0fa6983b-4c2c-5789-8e95-585a4e680b9e" :step/math-academy-id 172680 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152124 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151859 :question/id #uuid "960415f3-fe5d-53ea-a164-5960e800fb3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14642"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0075774-6bd8-54c9-b698-4aacd499fce5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049429}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523984}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Natural"} :step/id #uuid "bc685578-51b3-5ffb-b8b8-a5cca744297f" :step/math-academy-id 172681 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153004}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153004 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153466 :tutorial/content "When we extend the natural numbers by joining them with zero and the whole negative numbers, we get the **integers**.\n$\\overbrace{\\ldots, {\\color{red}-5},\\: {\\color{red}-4},\\: {\\color{red}-3},\\: {\\color{red}-2},\\: {\\color{red}-1},\\: {\\color{red}0}, \\: \\!\\underbrace{{\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2}, \\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots}_{\\large\\textrm{Natural numbers}}}^{\\large\\textrm{Integers}}$\n\nWe can represent the relationship between the natural numbers and integers using an inclusion diagram:\n\n![](images/00/00a2725bbe34eb8bc88f7f6dca6f22e624854a0cb469645eb2f4f9b79a38f846.png)\n\nNotice that the natural numbers are *contained within* the integers. In other words, *every* natural number is also an integer." :tutorial/id #uuid "4109da7f-f9b5-5c94-8215-06f5ea408038" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9070 :tutorial/title "The Integers"} :step/id #uuid "bfd8720b-23e8-55fe-8c02-ac787976007a" :step/math-academy-id 172684 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153005}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153005 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152125 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151860 :question/id #uuid "30b0a464-f903-5ee1-b649-97bfb366a73b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14643"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebf3f930-2768-5399-8470-48ac430065a3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524970}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Integers"} :step/id #uuid "34d04639-2557-5679-9ab1-fa22b4733ce3" :step/math-academy-id 172685 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153006}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153467 :tutorial/content "The **rational numbers** consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers. For example:\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{2}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nEvery integer is also a rational number because every integer can be expressed as a ratio of two integers. For example,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{2}{1}}, \\qquad {\\color{red}-9} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{(-9)}{\\phantom{-}1}}\n$$\n\nLikewise, any mixed number is a rational number because it can be written as an improper fraction. For example,\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nis a rational number because\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2 \\times 3 + 1}{3} = \\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the rational numbers to our inclusion diagram.\n\n![](images/12/124ced6b317bdd2df4f967e6ea45e613b89d9fa136acb5edf8f7ed86fa8a4c06.png)\n\nNotice that the integers (and therefore the natural numbers) are contained within the rational numbers." :tutorial/id #uuid "3563ca37-8d82-5742-92b2-bb3918a71477" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9071 :tutorial/title "The Rational Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "cc715ab1-8b8b-555b-bd73-97d8d33107c0" :step/math-academy-id 172688 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152126 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151861 :question/id #uuid "471f680f-5a51-5dac-aa3c-834610690f0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14644"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7063140-eebe-598c-b530-3c13509d359b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531609}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Rational"} :step/id #uuid "ba79c281-b939-528f-8605-0a6cd2aa6abd" :step/math-academy-id 172689 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153468 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following rational number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nWe can find the decimal representation of this rational number by dividing $1$ by $3$ using long division, as follows:\n\n| $\\,\\,0 \\,. 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3$$\\:\\ldots$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$\\,\\ldots$ |\n| --- |\n\nNote that, in this case\n\n- the long division process never ends, and\n- the digit $3$ after the decimal point repeats *forever*.\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13 = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nThis is an example of a **repeating decimal**. A repeating decimal is a decimal where the digits after the decimal point continue forever but in a repeating pattern.\n\nIt's convenient to use the following special notation to indicate decimals that repeat indefinitely.\n\n$$\n0.\\overline{3} = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nSome decimals have two or more digits that repeat indefinitely. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.78^(―) &= 0.78787878… \\\\\n0.675^(―) &= 0.675675675…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSome repeating decimals have non-repeating digits before the repeating part. For example:\n\n$$\n0.333\\overline{67} = 0.333\\,67\\,67\\,67\\,67\\ldots\n$$\n\nThe key takeaway for this section is that every repeating decimal is rational! In other words, every repeating decimal can be expressed as a ratio of two integers.\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.3^(―) &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n0.78^(―) &= \\frac{26}{33} \\\\\n0.33367^(―) &= \\frac{16517}{49500}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn a future lesson, we'll learn how to express a repeating decimal as a fraction. For now, you can verify these results using a calculator." :tutorial/id #uuid "28ff4afd-e8c7-511c-86a4-14d547e6c6d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9072 :tutorial/title "Repeating Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "8cd64bc9-e39e-5f5c-9c0d-618b3294cff3" :step/math-academy-id 172692 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153009}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152127 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151862 :question/id #uuid "f0bf7717-e639-5889-92fe-7cdcce737a55" :question/math-academy-id "e-14645"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be826645-6351-54ab-b57d-2c2a83a1b68d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050724}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152676}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rational Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "7c946365-2b4a-5987-84b7-84cce8c8a6f4" :step/math-academy-id 172693}] :activity/title "Natural Numbers, Integers, and Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152314}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433611} :activity/id #uuid "92c46132-b219-52cf-940d-0d4674e4370a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049935 :topic/id #uuid "1fcf3c5f-af9e-5e5e-b7aa-ff9b3a312835" :topic/math-academy-id 2558} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "415659c8-ed57-5ad3-a27e-b7733ecf22bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453916} {:answer/id #uuid "92089b13-bdb4-5085-b38e-26d2ce7b7510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453917}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453916} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4ca25b6-8beb-50ee-95d4-b2585267232c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454708} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dde4d56-adff-5ffe-b182-72bcc60b2763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453918} {:answer/id #uuid "93299e16-4673-5a0a-bb3e-c8c38ed2ade4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453919}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453918} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b18629e-b61d-552c-b62e-a2d1e0b590a7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454709} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5a5574c-eb8a-58f3-b104-88389929fd1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453920} {:answer/id #uuid "65e0b7ba-eb7d-5748-b3f5-a76e412f1f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453921} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb1dcca6-d76c-5952-ab4a-38f43ed8d5a5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "39fe4419-4a43-50fa-a07c-6ab5298bae4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-67819" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{1}{10}$ is natural number\n\n- **II.** $\\frac{1}{10}$ is an integer\n\n- **III.** $\\frac{1}{10}$ is a rational number" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $\\frac{1}{10}$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $\\frac{1}{10}$ is not an integer.\n\n- $\\frac{1}{10}$ is a rational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "44dc1f4c-fd4e-52ad-a63a-2ecc1e1041de" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf08aba8-f0da-51fd-ad95-8655627dd121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475014} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0aa0db-a2a7-5129-a388-9c57ac3f39d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475015} :answer-field/id #uuid "227c914e-b720-542d-b308-cc175cdab060" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475433} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99c304dc-b34a-561c-b8ce-39248e0e4792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475016} {:answer/id #uuid "6c28208b-ec2d-5048-a2a3-e8269f75cacc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475017}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475017} :answer-field/id #uuid "b16204db-52e8-540f-a01c-bb228b5f48f6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475434} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbd0222f-711f-5ac3-96b9-d66574a77070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475018} {:answer/id #uuid "63cd09e1-6bb0-5cf1-8595-a1e09adda1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475019}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475018} :answer-field/id #uuid "0073c129-f377-5688-a949-462c8471b3d9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eb2e9e07-c134-569e-a208-041621377fa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-99311" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are integers?\n\nI. $100$\n\nII. $-5$\n\nIII. $-\\frac{9}{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The integers consist of all positive and negative whole numbers and zero.\n\n$$\n…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…\n$$\n\n \n\nOf the given numbers, $100$ and $-5$ are integers, but $-\\frac{9}{2}$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "63f2e9a0-ccd8-53e1-823a-f93d8c2355e1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b64885-2bbd-537a-8370-5a0fdd5a797e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454096} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d6ce83-198f-5020-ae74-adf1f4a11fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454097}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454096} :answer-field/id #uuid "63e80334-99c3-51a1-a768-20f667e1870d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454798} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e253825-73c3-528c-a9c3-17c1030fe9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454098} {:answer/id #uuid "e4da0c49-de98-52dc-9c22-c42bfe19ff85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454099} :answer-field/id #uuid "320957d4-49dc-58ff-8a13-8882444c15f9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454799} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f564e66-c448-5603-aa7b-b58b98664d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454100} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4e5ec0-0acd-5415-bec0-f20a01d70ccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454101}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454101} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc111e4f-9395-590b-8835-f911d5c47ee3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "46c072ed-28fe-5e0e-96d0-a316d330f872" :question/math-academy-id "q-93611" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-10$ is a natural number\n\n- **II.** $-10$ is an integer\n\n- **III.** $-10$ is a rational number" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $-10$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $-10$ is an integer.\n\n- $-10$ is a rational number because it can be written as $\\frac{(-10)}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "678e552d-7057-5896-a457-bfa4af875ece" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ded51ae-3828-55c2-9e45-b194a3a30cce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448858} {:answer/id #uuid "c3fc0999-add2-5a61-99f1-345783abd7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448859}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448858} :answer-field/id #uuid "37f8f7af-8745-5efd-9267-e8a1ca839c