$-2$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $-2$ is an integer.\n\n- $-2$ is a rational number because it can be written as $\\frac{(-2)}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9656a63a-73b0-53ca-9b94-ede50eec4768" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a7a4510-1d8a-5ae4-9117-2759f14527f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475008} {:answer/id #uuid "05e2564a-26ec-53b1-9105-eef524306174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475009}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475008} :answer-field/id #uuid "887a1f56-eea6-509d-8bfd-0be97a597225" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475430} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3cdd3b1-f109-570f-aab0-c8a8042f40bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475010} {:answer/id #uuid "280a7746-17a8-5594-b498-4be355a8ebef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475011} :answer-field/id #uuid "86058e47-6f9f-51fd-a5ca-34c5d06d53ba" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475431} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "650b8815-0b38-51aa-9b3e-2e0ad39f65ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475012} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfbeb04-57b2-57b0-b551-d27531626ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475013} :answer-field/id #uuid "efdf8fd4-315b-58b9-97ad-de5cbc0003e8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "de7db80c-f818-55cb-b186-c1d21190ea48" :question/math-academy-id "q-98369" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are integers?\n\nI. $\\frac{3}{2}$\n\nII. $-3$\n\nIII. $5$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The integers consist of all positive and negative whole numbers and zero.\n\n$$\n…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…\n$$\n\n \n\nOf the given numbers, $-3$ and $5$ are integers, but $\\frac{3}{2}$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "c7a1ce99-411c-547f-b9eb-715734f5b768" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433614}}] :activity/title "Natural Numbers, Integers, and Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433582}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065089} :activity/id #uuid "7d552a48-87b4-5e70-9852-ea30a169d751" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049948 :topic/id #uuid "208ad900-2568-5e64-a594-cff48ca19048" :topic/math-academy-id 299} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065361 :tutorial/content "Multiplying any number by zero *always* equals zero. Below are some examples.\n\n- $5 \\times 0 = 0$\n- $123\\times 0 = 0$\n- $-314\\,159 \\times 0 = 0$\n- $0\\times 0 = 0$\n\nTo see *why* multiplying any number by zero always equals zero, let's take a look at the multiplication table for $5$, shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 \\cdot 4 &= 20 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 3 &= 15 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 2 &= 10 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 1 &= 5 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 0 &= ◻\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is reduced by $5$ each time. Following this pattern, we must have\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}\\square = 0\n$$\n\n.\n\nNote that, since multiplication is commutative, multiplying zero by any number (including itself) equals zero as well. Some examples are shown below.\n\n- $0 \\times 5 = 0$\n- $0 \\times 123 = 0$\n- $0 \\times -314\\,159 = 0$\n- $0 \\times 0 = 0$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7ed778da-8d5a-5363-972b-d9435e22a0e1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3119 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "77ea407f-55c3-5258-ba3f-15c16ab12b9a" :step/math-academy-id 16956 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065090}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064228 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064086 :question/id #uuid "078b5c64-d765-52da-9d87-56c23618c6a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4635"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42032d61-c2d5-5154-9a9d-d93e69d1ba53" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064923}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Number by Zero"} :step/id #uuid "32f923a1-966b-5c2d-aa49-a238ba8d46c2" :step/math-academy-id 16957 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065091}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065363 :tutorial/content "Dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero. For example:\n\n- $0 \\div 2 = 0$\n- $0\\div (-456) = 0$\n\nTo understand why dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero, think about it this way: if you have zero pizzas, and you want to divide zero pizzas among ten friends, then each friend will get exactly zero pizza." :tutorial/id #uuid "65c0e3ec-7962-5911-a3c9-9de126e87edc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3120 :tutorial/title "Dividing Zero by Another Number"} :step/id #uuid "cb642d1f-f4bb-5b57-be91-7221332ef04b" :step/math-academy-id 16959 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065092}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064229 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064087 :question/id #uuid "897fedd3-db36-5614-886c-c88fba708692" :question/math-academy-id "e-4637"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2097574-c45c-504d-8ec8-44474880cd21" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525050}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Zero by a Number"} :step/id #uuid "28f3ce5f-977b-550b-9bda-7ae39c91c330" :step/math-academy-id 16960 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064230 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064088 :question/id #uuid "800d4367-20e6-55a9-8469-7c3654d512a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4638"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3df16e28-70ed-5dd0-9bf0-d56b38f542eb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524035}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Zero by a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "a914b898-5054-5bf0-b58e-1526eea6a0fa" :step/math-academy-id 16961 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065094}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065364 :tutorial/content "Division by zero is **undefined.** No number can be divided by zero, not even zero itself.\n\nTo see why, notice that if we divide $6$ by $3$, we get\n\n$$\n6\\div 3 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n6 = 3\\times 2\n$$\n\nHowever, this argument does not work if we divide by zero. Suppose we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThen, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nand this relationship cannot be true because any number multiplied by zero is zero! Therefore, $$? is not a number." :tutorial/id #uuid "d8152649-f215-53b2-9811-ce74f0d61dc3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3121 :tutorial/title "Dividing by Zero is Undefined"} :step/id #uuid "4